== Samples and populations
<samples-and-populations-1>
The #strong[population] is everyone the conclusions are meant to cover: here, graduate students. The #strong[sample] is the people actually measured, the 600 students of the wellbeing study. A number that describes the population, such as the true average sleep of all graduate students, is a #strong[parameter]\; it is never known exactly. The same number calculated from the sample is a #strong[statistic], and it serves as the estimate of the parameter.

A second sample would give a slightly different estimate, and a third another. The size of this variation cannot be seen in a single study, but it can be seen in a simulation. For a moment, treat the 600 students as if they were the whole population, draw many small samples from them, and watch how the estimates behave.

=== Sampling distributions
<sampling-distributions>
Caffeine intake is strongly skewed (Chapter 6): most students take a moderate amount, and a few take a great deal. The simulation draws a sample of 5 students and records their average caffeine, repeats this a thousand times, and then does the same with samples of 30.

In Python, we use NumPy's random number generator #NormalTok("np.random.default_rng()"); for reproducible simulations:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("first_sem ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" (");],
[#NormalTok("    semesters.query(");#StringTok("\"semester == 1\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    .merge(students, on");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"student_id\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("caffeine ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" first_sem[");#StringTok("\"caffeine_mg\"");#NormalTok("].dropna().to_numpy()");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# Fix seed for reproducible random draws");],
[#NormalTok("rng ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.random.default_rng(");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# Draw 1,000 samples of size 5 and size 30");],
[#NormalTok("means_5 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.array([rng.choice(caffeine, size");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("5");#NormalTok(", replace");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("True");#NormalTok(").mean() ");#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" _ ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1000");#NormalTok(")])");],
[#NormalTok("means_30 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.array([rng.choice(caffeine, size");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("30");#NormalTok(", replace");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("True");#NormalTok(").mean() ");#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" _ ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1000");#NormalTok(")])");],));
]
The thousand averages form a #strong[sampling distribution]: the distribution of a statistic over many possible samples. #ref(<fig-clt>, supplement: [Figure]) compares the sampling distributions for samples of 5 and of 30 students.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("fig, (ax1, ax2) ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" plt.subplots(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", figsize");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.4");#NormalTok("), sharey");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("True");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("sns.histplot(means_5, bins");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("35");#NormalTok(", color");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"#b0bec5\"");#NormalTok(", edgecolor");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"white\"");#NormalTok(", ax");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("ax1)");],
[#NormalTok("ax1.set_title(");#StringTok("\"Samples of 5 (Widely Spread & Skewed)\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax1.set_xlabel(");#StringTok("\"Sample Mean Caffeine (mg)\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax1.set_ylabel(");#StringTok("\"Number of Samples\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("sns.histplot(means_30, bins");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("35");#NormalTok(", color");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"#1d588c\"");#NormalTok(", edgecolor");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"white\"");#NormalTok(", ax");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("ax2)");],
[#NormalTok("ax2.set_title(");#StringTok("\"Samples of 30 (Narrow & Bell-Shaped)\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax2.set_xlabel(");#StringTok("\"Sample Mean Caffeine (mg)\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("sns.despine()");],
[#NormalTok("plt.tight_layout()");],
[#NormalTok("plt.show()");],));
#figure([
#box(image("07-hypothesis-testing_files/figure-typst/fig-clt-output-1.svg", alt: "Two histograms side by side. For samples of 5, the averages are widely spread and skewed to the right. For samples of 30, they are narrow and nearly symmetrical, centred on the same value."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
The sampling distribution of average caffeine intake, for samples of 5 and of 30 students drawn from the student wellbeing data.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-clt>


The figure demonstrates the foundational pillars of statistical inference:

+ #strong[Unbiasedness]: Both distributions are centred on the average of all 600 students (np.float64(189.4) mg). A random sample average neither systematically overestimates nor underestimates the true population parameter.
+ #strong[Precision]: The averages of 30 students are packed far more tightly than the averages of 5. Larger sample sizes reduce random sampling variability.
+ #strong[The Central Limit Theorem (CLT)]: Although raw caffeine intake is heavily right-skewed, the sample means for $n = 30$ form a symmetrical, bell-shaped distribution. The CLT guarantees that regardless of the underlying population's shape, the sampling distribution of the mean approaches normality as $n$ increases. As an empirical rule of thumb, $n gt.eq 30$ provides sufficient normality for inference unless the underlying distribution is severely pathological.

=== The standard error
<the-standard-error>
The spread of a sampling distribution is called the #strong[standard error] (SE). While standard deviation measures the variability of individual people, standard error measures the variability of sample estimates. A real study measures only one sample and cannot draw a thousand, but the standard error of a sample mean can be computed directly from that single sample:

$ S E = s / sqrt(n) $

where $s$ is the sample standard deviation and $n$ is the sample size. We can compare the theoretical formula with our simulation:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("simulated_se ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" means_30.std(ddof");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("formula_se ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" caffeine.std(ddof");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" np.sqrt(");#DecValTok("30");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Simulated SE (spread of 1,000 means): ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("simulated_se");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok(" mg\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Theoretical SE (s / sqrt(n)):          ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("formula_se");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok(" mg\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Simulated SE (spread of 1,000 means): 26.88 mg");],
[#NormalTok("Theoretical SE (s / sqrt(n)):          26.22 mg");],));
]
]
The formula and simulation agree closely. Because the denominator is $sqrt(n)$, halving the uncertainty of an estimate requires #emph[quadrupling] the sample size.

=== Confidence intervals
<confidence-intervals>
A point estimate on its own conveys no information about its precision. A #strong[confidence interval] (CI) supplies that context: a range of plausible values for the population parameter, given the sample data. An approximate 95% confidence interval for a mean is:

$ macron(x) plus.minus t^(*) times S E $

where $t^(*)$ is the critical value from Student's $t$-distribution with $n - 1$ degrees of freedom (close to 1.96 for large samples).

In SciPy, we calculate this interval using #NormalTok("scipy.stats.t.interval"); or directly via #NormalTok("scipy.stats.ttest_1samp");:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("sleep_data ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" first_sem[");#StringTok("\"sleep_hours\"");#NormalTok("].dropna()");],
[#NormalTok("n_sleep ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("len");#NormalTok("(sleep_data)");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_mean ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sleep_data.mean()");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_se ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sleep_data.std(ddof");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" np.sqrt(n_sleep)");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# Calculate 95% Confidence Interval using SciPy");],
[#NormalTok("ci_low, ci_high ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.t.interval(");],
[#NormalTok("    confidence");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("0.95");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    df");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("n_sleep ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    loc");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("sleep_mean,");],
[#NormalTok("    scale");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("sleep_se");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Sample Mean:  ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sleep_mean");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok(" hours\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"95% CI:       [");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("ci_low");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok(", ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("ci_high");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("] hours\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Sample Mean:  6.48 hours");],
[#NormalTok("95% CI:       [6.40, 6.57] hours");],));
]
]
The average sleep of graduate students is plausibly between 6.40 and 6.57 hours per night. The interval is narrow because our sample ($N = 600$) is large.

