=== Bootstrap confidence intervals
<bootstrap-confidence-intervals>
The analytical formula above works for means. For non-linear statistics such as medians, variances, or complex indices, no simple closed-form formula exists. The #strong[bootstrap] solves this with an idea of brilliant computational simplicity: treat the observed sample as a proxy for the population, and draw thousands of resamples #emph[with replacement] (each resample has size $n$, containing duplicate participants and omitting others). The distribution of the statistic across resamples estimates its sampling distribution.

Suppose we collected caffeine intake from only 40 students, and need a 95% confidence interval for their #strong[median] intake:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("rng ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.random.default_rng(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("small_sample ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" rng.choice(caffeine, size");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("40");#NormalTok(", replace");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("False");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("sample_median ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.median(small_sample)");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# Generate 2,000 bootstrap resamples with replacement");],
[#NormalTok("boot_medians ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.array([");],
[#NormalTok("    np.median(rng.choice(small_sample, size");#OperatorTok("=");#BuiltInTok("len");#NormalTok("(small_sample), replace");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("True");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" _ ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2000");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("])");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# 95% Percentile Bootstrap Interval");],
[#NormalTok("boot_ci_low ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.percentile(boot_medians, ");#FloatTok("2.5");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("boot_ci_high ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.percentile(boot_medians, ");#FloatTok("97.5");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Sample Median:        ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sample_median");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok(" mg\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"95% Bootstrap CI:     [");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("boot_ci_low");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok(", ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("boot_ci_high");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok("] mg\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Sample Median:        147.5 mg");],
[#NormalTok("95% Bootstrap CI:     [127.5, 180.1] mg");],));
]
]
The true population median across all 600 students (np.float64(160.0) mg) falls cleanly inside this bootstrap interval. In modern Python, SciPy also provides #NormalTok("scipy.stats.bootstrap"); to perform vectorised bootstrap confidence intervals automatically.

== The logic of hypothesis testing
<the-logic-of-hypothesis-testing>
A confidence interval describes which values are plausible. A #strong[hypothesis test] addresses a sharper question: whether the observed data is consistent with a specific theoretical claim.

The claim being evaluated is the #strong[null hypothesis] ($H_0$): the statement of "no effect", "no difference", or "no association". The researcher's substantive claim is the #strong[alternative hypothesis] ($H_1$). For the wellbeing workshop: - $H_0$: The workshop has zero effect on semester 2 student wellbeing ($mu_(upright("invited")) - mu_(upright("control")) = 0$). - $H_1$: The workshop changes student wellbeing ($mu_(upright("invited")) - mu_(upright("control")) eq.not 0$).

Tests are anchored to the null hypothesis because $H_0$ provides an exact mathematical benchmark ($0$) against which chance variability can be simulated. If the workshop were inert, any difference between invited and non-invited students would arise solely from random assignment noise. We can simulate that chance mechanism directly.

=== A permutation test
<a-permutation-test>
We examine students' wellbeing at the conclusion of semester 2, comparing those invited to the workshop with those not invited:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("semester2 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" (");],
[#NormalTok("    semesters.query(");#StringTok("\"semester == 2\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    .merge(students, on");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"student_id\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("mean_invited ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" semester2.query(");#StringTok("\"workshop == 'Invited'\"");#NormalTok(")[");#StringTok("\"wellbeing\"");#NormalTok("].mean()");],
[#NormalTok("mean_control ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" semester2.query(");#StringTok("\"workshop == 'Not invited'\"");#NormalTok(")[");#StringTok("\"wellbeing\"");#NormalTok("].mean()");],
[#NormalTok("observed_gap ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" mean_invited ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" mean_control");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Invited Mean:     ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("mean_invited");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Not Invited Mean: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("mean_control");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Observed Gap:     ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("observed_gap");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok(" points\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Invited Mean:     65.47");],
[#NormalTok("Not Invited Mean: 60.22");],
[#NormalTok("Observed Gap:     5.25 points");],));
]
]
Invited students scored 5.25 points higher on average. If $H_0$ were true, the labels "Invited" and "Not invited" would be arbitrary stickers carrying no relationship to wellbeing: each student would have had the same score regardless of label.

Under $H_0$, we can randomly shuffle the workshop labels across students 5,000 times, recalculating the group difference each time to build the empirical #strong[null distribution]:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("rng ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.random.default_rng(");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
