=== Type I and Type II errors
<type-i-and-type-ii-errors>
Because inference relies on probabilities, any decision can be mistaken in two distinct ways:

#figure([
#table(
  columns: (33.33%, 33.33%, 33.33%),
  align: (auto,auto,auto,),
  table.header([], [$H_0$ is True in Reality], [$H_0$ is False in Reality],),
  table.hline(),
  [#strong[Decision: Reject $H_0$]], [#strong[Type I error] (False alarm, rate $alpha$)], [Correct decision (Power, $1 - beta$)],
  [#strong[Decision: Retain $H_0$]], [Correct decision ($1 - alpha$)], [#strong[Type II error] (Missed effect, rate $beta$)],
)
], caption: figure.caption(
position: top, 
[
The decision matrix in statistical hypothesis testing
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-tbl", 
supplement: "Table", 
)
<tbl-errors>


The significance level $alpha$ sets the Type I error rate. If we test a true null hypothesis repeatedly at $alpha = 0.05$, exactly 5% of tests will be "significant" by pure chance. We can demonstrate this by splitting students into two random dummy groups 1,000 times:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("rng ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.random.default_rng(");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("wellbeing_data ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" first_sem[");#StringTok("\"wellbeing\"");#NormalTok("].to_numpy()");],
[],
[#NormalTok("false_alarms ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" []");],
[#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" _ ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1000");#NormalTok("):");],
[#NormalTok("    perm ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" rng.permutation(wellbeing_data)");],
[#NormalTok("    grp_a, grp_b ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" perm[:");#DecValTok("300");#NormalTok("], perm[");#DecValTok("300");#NormalTok(":]");],
[#NormalTok("    res ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.ttest_ind(grp_a, grp_b, equal_var");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("False");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    false_alarms.append(res.pvalue ");#OperatorTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.05");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Empirical Type I Error Rate: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("np");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("mean(false_alarms) ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("100");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok("%\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Empirical Type I Error Rate: 4.6%");],));
]
]
Approximately 5% of tests sound a false alarm. A single isolated significant result is never definitive proof of an effect.

=== Statistical power
<statistical-power>
#strong[Power] ($1 - beta$) is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis---that is, detecting a real effect when it genuinely exists. Power depends on three factors: 1. #strong[Sample size ($N$)]: Larger samples provide higher power. 2. #strong[True effect size ($delta$)]: Larger effects are easier to detect. 3. #strong[Significance threshold ($alpha$)]: A stricter threshold (e.g.~0.01) reduces power.

We can simulate how sample size affects the detection rate of a 5-point workshop effect ($S D = 11$):

