A study with only 10 students per group detects a real effect only \~17% of the time, almost always ending in a false negative. Detecting this effect with standard 80% power requires approximately 77 students per group. Our study's 300 students per group provides $> 99 %$ power.

=== Multiple testing
<multiple-testing>
When running multiple statistical tests simultaneously, false alarm risks compound rapidly. For $k$ independent tests at $alpha = 0.05$, the probability of at least one false positive (#strong[family-wise error rate], FWER) is:

$ upright("FWER") = 1 - \( 1 - alpha \)^k $

For $k = 20$ tests, FWER is $1 - 0.95^20 approx 64.2 %$. Over 60% of studies running 20 tests on pure noise will report a "statistically significant" finding:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("rng ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.random.default_rng(");#DecValTok("8");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("any_false ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" []");],
[#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" _ ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1000");#NormalTok("):");],
[#NormalTok("    p_set ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" [stats.ttest_ind(rng.normal(size");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("30");#NormalTok("), rng.normal(size");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("30");#NormalTok(")).pvalue ");#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" _ ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("20");#NormalTok(")]");],
[#NormalTok("    any_false.append(");#BuiltInTok("any");#NormalTok("(p ");#OperatorTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.05");#NormalTok(" ");#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" p ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" p_set))");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Empirical FWER for 20 tests: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("np");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("mean(any_false) ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("100");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok("%\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Empirical FWER for 20 tests: 64.0%");],));
]
]
To control false alarms across multiple tests, researchers apply corrections such as #strong[Bonferroni] ($alpha \/ k$) or Benjamini-Hochberg False Discovery Rate via #NormalTok("statsmodels.stats.multitest.multipletests");.

== The one-sample t-test
<the-one-sample-t-test>
Research Question 2 asks whether graduate students sleep less than the recommended 7 hours. A #strong[one-sample $t$-test] evaluates whether a sample mean differs significantly from a hypothesized value $mu_0$:

$ t = frac(macron(x) - mu_0, s \/ sqrt(n)) $

In SciPy, we call #NormalTok("scipy.stats.ttest_1samp");:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("sleep_test ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.ttest_1samp(first_sem[");#StringTok("\"sleep_hours\"");#NormalTok("].dropna(), popmean");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("7.0");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ci ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sleep_test.confidence_interval(confidence_level");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("0.95");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"t-statistic: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sleep_test");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("statistic");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"df:          ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sleep_test");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("df");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"p-value:     ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sleep_test");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("pvalue");#SpecialCharTok(":.2e}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"95% CI:      [");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("ci");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("low");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok(", ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("ci");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("high");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("] hours\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("t-statistic: -12.18");],
[#NormalTok("df:          593");],
[#NormalTok("p-value:     1.33e-30");],
[#NormalTok("95% CI:      [6.40, 6.57] hours");],));
]
]
Graduate students sleep an average of 6.48 hours per night, which is significantly below the 7-hour threshold ($t \( 595 \) = - 11.96 \, p < .001$). The 95% confidence interval shows that students sleep between 24 and 38 minutes less than recommended each night.

== The two-sample t-test (Welch's t-test)
<the-two-sample-t-test-welchs-t-test>
Research Question 3 compares the semester 2 wellbeing of students randomly invited to the workshop against those not invited.

A #strong[two-sample $t$-test] compares the means of two independent groups. By default, #NormalTok("stats.ttest_ind"); assumes equal variances. Setting #NormalTok("equal_var=False"); performs #strong[Welch's $t$-test], which adjusts degrees of freedom for unequal group variances and is standard practice across scientific disciplines:

