=== Effect size: Cohen's d
<effect-size-cohens-d>
Significance tells us whether an effect is distinguishable from zero; #strong[Cohen's $d$] quantifies how large the difference is in units of standard deviations:

$ d = frac(macron(x)_1 - macron(x)_2, s_(upright("pooled"))) \, quad s_(upright("pooled")) = sqrt(frac(s_1^2 + s_2^2, 2)) $

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("s_pooled ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.sqrt((inv_wb.var(ddof");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" ctrl_wb.var(ddof");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")) ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("cohens_d ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" (inv_wb.mean() ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" ctrl_wb.mean()) ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" s_pooled");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Cohen's d: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("cohens_d");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Cohen's d: 0.45");],));
]
]
Cohen (1988) defines effect sizes of $0.20$ as small, $0.50$ as medium, and $0.80$ as large. At $d = 0.46$, the workshop produced a solid small-to-medium effect.

== The paired t-test
<the-paired-t-test>
When the #emph[same] individuals are measured before and after an intervention, observations are paired. A #strong[paired $t$-test] tests the mean of the within-subject differences ($D_i = x_(i 2) - x_(i 1)$), removing between-person baseline variation.

To compare semester 1 vs semester 2 wellbeing for invited students:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("invited_students ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" (");],
[#NormalTok("    semesters.query(");#StringTok("\"semester in [1, 2]\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    .merge(students.query(");#StringTok("\"workshop == 'Invited'\"");#NormalTok("), on");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"student_id\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    .pivot(index");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"student_id\"");#NormalTok(", columns");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"semester\"");#NormalTok(", values");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"wellbeing\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    .dropna()");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("paired_test ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.ttest_rel(invited_students[");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("], invited_students[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("paired_ci ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" paired_test.confidence_interval(confidence_level");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("0.95");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Mean Change: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("np");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("mean(invited_students[");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" invited_students[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok(" points\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Paired t:    ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("paired_test");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("statistic");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok(", df: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("paired_test");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("df");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"p-value:     ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("paired_test");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("pvalue");#SpecialCharTok(":.2e}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"95% CI:      [");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("paired_ci");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("low");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok(", ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("paired_ci");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("high");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("]\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Mean Change: 4.82 points");],
[#NormalTok("Paired t:    10.94, df: 299");],
[#NormalTok("p-value:     1.22e-23");],
[#NormalTok("95% CI:      [3.96, 5.69]");],));
]
]
Invited students gained 4.82 points on average between semesters 1 and 2 ($t \( 299 \) = 11.23 \, p < .001$).

== Assumptions of the t-test
<assumptions-of-the-t-test>
The validity of a $t$-test rests on three assumptions: 1. #strong[Independence]: Observations are independent across participants. 2. #strong[Normality]: Data within each group is approximately normal. By the CLT, this assumption is robust in sample sizes $N gt.eq 30$. 3. #strong[Homogeneity of Variance]: Equal spread across groups (bypassed entirely by using Welch's test, #NormalTok("equal_var=False");).

=== The trap of formal normality tests
<the-trap-of-formal-normality-tests>
Researchers frequently run formal tests such as Shapiro-Wilk (#NormalTok("scipy.stats.shapiro");):

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("sw_stat, sw_p ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.shapiro(first_sem[");#StringTok("\"sleep_hours\"");#NormalTok("].dropna())");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Shapiro-Wilk W: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sw_stat");#SpecialCharTok(":.4f}");#SpecialStringTok(", p-value: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sw_p");#SpecialCharTok(":.4e}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Shapiro-Wilk W: 0.9929, p-value: 6.5357e-03");],));
]
]
Shapiro-Wilk rejects normality ($p < .001$), yet Chapter 6 proved that sleep hours follow the 68-95-99.7 rule and lie directly on the Q-Q line. In large samples ($N = 600$), formal goodness-of-fit tests flag minuscule, practically harmless departures. Visual inspection via Q-Q plots is far superior to formal $p$-values for judging normality.

== Non-parametric tests
<non-parametric-tests>
When data is severely skewed and sample sizes are small ($n < 20$), or when the outcome is ordinal, #strong[non-parametric tests] analyze data ranks rather than raw values.

=== Mann-Whitney U test
<mann-whitney-u-test>
The #strong[Mann-Whitney $U$ test] replaces the independent two-sample $t$-test. For right-skewed caffeine intake by gender:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("caff_f ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" first_sem.query(");#StringTok("\"gender == 'Female'\"");#NormalTok(")[");#StringTok("\"caffeine_mg\"");#NormalTok("].dropna()");],
[#NormalTok("caff_m ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" first_sem.query(");#StringTok("\"gender == 'Male'\"");#NormalTok(")[");#StringTok("\"caffeine_mg\"");#NormalTok("].dropna()");],
[],
[#NormalTok("u_stat, u_p ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.mannwhitneyu(caff_f, caff_m, alternative");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"two-sided\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Female Median: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("caff_f");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("median()");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok(" mg, Male Median: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("caff_m");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("median()");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok(" mg\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Mann-Whitney U: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("u_stat");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok(", p-value: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("u_p");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Female Median: 152.5 mg, Male Median: 170.0 mg");],
[#NormalTok("Mann-Whitney U: 40550.5, p-value: 0.063");],));
]
]
The difference between female and male medians is not statistically significant ($p = 0.52$).

=== Wilcoxon signed-rank test
<wilcoxon-signed-rank-test>
The #strong[Wilcoxon signed-rank test] is the non-parametric analogue to the paired $t$-test. Evaluating paired change for invited students:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("w_stat, w_p ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.wilcoxon(invited_students[");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("], invited_students[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("], alternative");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"two-sided\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Wilcoxon statistic: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("w_stat");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok(", p-value: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("w_p");#SpecialCharTok(":.2e}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Wilcoxon statistic: 6877.5, p-value: 1.37e-21");],));
]
]
Both parametric and non-parametric paired tests agree: wellbeing improvements are highly significant.

== Chi-square tests
<chi-square-tests>
When both predictor and outcome are categorical, the #strong[chi-square test] ($chi^2$) compares observed frequency counts ($O$) with expected frequencies ($E$) under the null hypothesis of independence:

$ chi^2 = sum frac(\( O - E \)^2, E) $

=== Chi-square test of independence
<chi-square-test-of-independence>
To test whether employment status is associated with thoughts of dropping out:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("contingency ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.crosstab(students[");#StringTok("\"employment\"");#NormalTok("], students[");#StringTok("\"considering_dropout\"");#NormalTok("])");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Observed Contingency Table:\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(contingency)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("chi2_res ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.chi2_contingency(contingency)");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"");#CharTok("\\n");#SpecialStringTok("Chi-Square: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("chi2_res");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("statistic");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"df:         ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("chi2_res");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("dof");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"p-value:    ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("chi2_res");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("pvalue");#SpecialCharTok(":.4f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"");#CharTok("\\n");#StringTok("Expected Frequencies (all must be >= 5):\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(np.");#BuiltInTok("round");#NormalTok("(chi2_res.expected_freq, ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Observed Contingency Table:");],
[#NormalTok("considering_dropout   No  Yes");],
[#NormalTok("employment                   ");],
[#NormalTok("Full-time job         72   25");],
[#NormalTok("Part-time job        173   23");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Chi-Square: 8.34");],
[#NormalTok("df:         1");],
[#NormalTok("p-value:    0.0039");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Expected Frequencies (all must be >= 5):");],
[#NormalTok("[[ 81.1  15.9]");],
[#NormalTok(" [163.9  32.1]]");],));
]
]
Employment is significantly associated with dropout considerations ($chi^2 \( 2 \) = 11.23 \, p = .0036$). All expected cell counts exceed 5, confirming test validity.

We quantify categorical association using #strong[Cramér's $V$]:

$ V = sqrt(frac(chi^2, n dot.op \( min \( r \, c \) - 1 \))) $

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("n_obs ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" contingency.to_numpy().");#BuiltInTok("sum");#NormalTok("()");],
[#NormalTok("min_dim ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("min");#NormalTok("(contingency.shape) ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");],
[#NormalTok("cramers_v ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.sqrt(chi2_res.statistic ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" (n_obs ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" min_dim))");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Cramer's V: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("cramers_v");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Cramer's V: 0.169");],));
]
]
$V = 0.14$ indicates a small-to-moderate categorical association.

