#strong[One-Sample $t$-test:] \> Graduate students slept significantly fewer hours than the clinically recommended 7 hours per night ($M = 6.48$, $S D = 1.07$, 95% CI $\[ 6.40 \, 6.57 \]$), $t \( 595 \) = - 11.96$, $p < .001$, representing an average deficit of 31 minutes per night.

#strong[Two-Sample Welch's $t$-test:] \> Students invited to the wellbeing workshop reported significantly higher wellbeing at the conclusion of semester 2 ($M = 58.74$, $S D = 10.96$) than non-invited peers ($M = 53.64$, $S D = 11.08$), mean difference = $5.10$ points, 95% CI $\[ 3.33 \, 6.87 \]$, $t \( 597.9 \) = 5.63$, $p < .001$, $d = 0.46$. A 5,000-resample permutation test yielded identical conclusions ($p < .001$). The null hypothesis of no intervention effect was rejected.

#strong[Chi-Square Test of Independence:] \> Considerations of programme withdrawal were significantly associated with employment status, $chi^2 \( 2 \) = 11.23$, $p = .004$, Cramér's $V = 0.14$. Specifically, 25.0% of students employed full-time considered dropping out, compared to only 12.5% of students without external employment.

