== One-way ANOVA
<one-way-anova>
The first question is whether stress differs between the five faculties. One approach would be a t-test for every pair of faculties, but that is 10 tests. Chapter 7 showed that each test has a 5% chance of a false alarm, and that the risks accumulate: across 10 independent tests, the chance of at least one false alarm is about 40%. #strong[Analysis of variance] (ANOVA) avoids the problem by asking one question first: whether the faculty averages differ #emph[at all].

=== Partitioning variation
<partitioning-variation>
ANOVA compares two kinds of variation. Stress scores for three small groups show the idea:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("group_scores ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.DataFrame({");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"group\"");#NormalTok(": [");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" [");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" [");#StringTok("\"C\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"stress\"");#NormalTok(": [");#FloatTok("2.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.1");#NormalTok(",   ");#FloatTok("3.4");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.9");#NormalTok(",   ");#FloatTok("2.9");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.3");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.4");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("})");],
[#NormalTok("group_scores.groupby(");#StringTok("\"group\"");#NormalTok(")[");#StringTok("\"stress\"");#NormalTok("].mean().reset_index()");],));
#table(
  columns: 3,
  align: (auto,auto,auto,),
  table.header(table.cell(align: right)[], table.cell(align: right)[group], table.cell(align: right)[stress],),
  table.hline(),
  table.cell(align: horizon)[0], [A], [2.85],
  table.cell(align: horizon)[1], [B], [3.65],
  table.cell(align: horizon)[2], [C], [3.15],
)
#ref(<fig-partition>, supplement: [Figure]) draws the twelve scores, the average of each group, and the overall average. The group averages differ from the overall average: that is variation #strong[between] groups. The scores also differ from their own group's average: that is variation #strong[within] groups.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("group_means ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" group_scores.groupby(");#StringTok("\"group\"");#NormalTok(")[");#StringTok("\"stress\"");#NormalTok("].mean()");],
[#NormalTok("grand_mean ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" group_scores[");#StringTok("\"stress\"");#NormalTok("].mean()");],
[],
[#NormalTok("fig, ax ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" plt.subplots(figsize");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("5.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.6");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("ax.axhline(grand_mean, color");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"grey\"");#NormalTok(", linestyle");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"--\"");#NormalTok(", linewidth");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("1.2");#NormalTok(", label");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"Grand Mean\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("x_map ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" {");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok(": ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok(": ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"C\"");#NormalTok(": ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("}");],
[#NormalTok("offsets ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" [");#OperatorTok("-");#FloatTok("0.09");#NormalTok(", ");#OperatorTok("-");#FloatTok("0.03");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("0.03");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("0.09");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("3");],
[],
[#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" i, row ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" group_scores.iterrows():");],
[#NormalTok("    ax.scatter(x_map[row[");#StringTok("\"group\"");#NormalTok("]] ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" offsets[i], row[");#StringTok("\"stress\"");#NormalTok("], color");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"#1d588c\"");#NormalTok(", s");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("45");#NormalTok(", zorder");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" g, mean_val ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" group_means.items():");],
[#NormalTok("    ax.hlines(mean_val, x_map[g] ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.25");#NormalTok(", x_map[g] ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.25");#NormalTok(", color");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"black\"");#NormalTok(", linewidth");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("2.5");#NormalTok(", zorder");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_xticks([");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_xticklabels([");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"C\"");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_xlabel(");#StringTok("\"Group\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_ylabel(");#StringTok("\"Stress score\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("plt.tight_layout()");],
[#NormalTok("plt.show()");],));
#figure([
#box(image("08-anova-regression_files/figure-typst/fig-partition-output-1.svg", alt: "Twelve points in three columns, A, B, and C. Each column has a short horizontal line at its average; group B's line is highest. A dashed horizontal line marks the overall average across the whole plot."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Variation between and within groups. Points are individual scores, short black lines are group averages, and the dashed line is the overall average. The gaps between the black lines and the dashed line are between-group variation; the spread of the points around their black line is within-group variation.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-partition>


If the groups really come from populations with the same average, the between-group variation should be no larger than the within-group variation would lead one to expect: group averages always differ a little by chance. ANOVA's #strong[F statistic] is the ratio of the two:

$ F = upright("variation between groups") / upright("variation within groups") $

An F close to 1 means that the group averages differ no more than chance would produce; a large F means that they differ more. In Python, we can fit a linear model using #NormalTok("statsmodels.formula.api.ols"); and compute the ANOVA table using #NormalTok("anova_lm");:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" statsmodels.api ");#ImportTok("as");#NormalTok(" sm");],
[#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" statsmodels.formula.api ");#ImportTok("as");#NormalTok(" smf");],
[],
[#NormalTok("model_group ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"stress ~ C(group)\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("group_scores).fit()");],
[#NormalTok("anova_table ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sm.stats.anova_lm(model_group, typ");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(anova_table)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("           df    sum_sq   mean_sq          F    PR(>F)");],
[#NormalTok("C(group)  2.0  1.306667  0.653333  10.690909  0.004191");],
[#NormalTok("Residual  9.0  0.550000  0.061111        NaN       NaN");],));
]
]
The p-value is small ($p = 0.004$): these three groups differ significantly.

The ratio matters more than the size of the differences between averages. The next code keeps the three group averages exactly as they are, but places the scores either close to their averages or far from them, and calculates F each time:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("offsets ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.tile([");#OperatorTok("-");#FloatTok("0.3");#NormalTok(", ");#OperatorTok("-");#FloatTok("0.1");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("0.1");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("0.3");#NormalTok("], ");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("group_avg ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.repeat(group_means.values, ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("tight ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" group_scores.copy()");],
[#NormalTok("tight[");#StringTok("\"stress\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" group_avg ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.5");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" offsets");],
[],
[#NormalTok("wide ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" group_scores.copy()");],
[#NormalTok("wide[");#StringTok("\"stress\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" group_avg ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("4.0");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" offsets");],
[],
[#NormalTok("f_tight ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sm.stats.anova_lm(smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"stress ~ C(group)\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("tight).fit(), typ");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")[");#StringTok("\"F\"");#NormalTok("].iloc[");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("f_wide ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sm.stats.anova_lm(smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"stress ~ C(group)\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("wide).fit(), typ");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")[");#StringTok("\"F\"");#NormalTok("].iloc[");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok("]");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"tight: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("f_tight");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok(", wide: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("f_wide");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tight: 39.20, wide: 0.61");],));
]
]
The group averages are identical in both versions, yet F is large when the scores cluster tightly around their averages and small when they spread widely. Differences of about half a point between groups are convincing when students within a group hardly differ, and unremarkable when they differ by more than two points. This is the whole logic of ANOVA, and it is why group averages should never be compared without looking at the spread within groups (Chapter 4).

=== The student wellbeing data
<the-student-wellbeing-data>
The data comes first. #ref(<fig-stress-faculty>, supplement: [Figure]) shows stress in each faculty.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("fig, ax ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" plt.subplots(figsize");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.8");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("sns.boxplot(");],
[#NormalTok("    data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("study,");],
[#NormalTok("    x");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"faculty\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    y");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"stress\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    color");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"#eaf0f7\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    width");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("0.5");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    linewidth");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("1.0");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    ax");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("ax");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_xlabel(");#StringTok("\"\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_ylabel(");#StringTok("\"Stress score (1 to 5)\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("plt.xticks(rotation");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("15");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("plt.tight_layout()");],
[#NormalTok("plt.show()");],));
#figure([
#box(image("08-anova-regression_files/figure-typst/fig-stress-faculty-output-1.svg", alt: "Five box plots of stress scores, one per faculty. Health Sciences is highest and Humanities lowest, with much overlap between all faculties."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Stress scores by faculty.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-stress-faculty>


The faculties overlap a lot, but Health Sciences sits a little higher and Humanities a little lower. The ANOVA tests whether these differences exceed what chance would produce:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("stress_anova ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"stress ~ C(faculty)\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("study).fit()");],
[#NormalTok("anova_summary ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sm.stats.anova_lm(stress_anova, typ");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(anova_summary)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("               df      sum_sq   mean_sq         F    PR(>F)");],
[#NormalTok("C(faculty)    4.0    8.715036  2.178759  4.152488  0.002516");],
[#NormalTok("Residual    595.0  312.189093  0.524688       NaN       NaN");],));
]
]
The p-value is $0.003$, so the faculties do differ in stress. The size of the difference is measured by #strong[eta squared] ($eta^2$), the share of all the variation in stress that lies between faculties:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("ss_between ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" anova_summary.loc[");#StringTok("\"C(faculty)\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"sum_sq\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("ss_total ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" anova_summary[");#StringTok("\"sum_sq\"");#NormalTok("].");#BuiltInTok("sum");#NormalTok("()");],
[#NormalTok("eta_sq ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ss_between ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" ss_total");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Eta squared: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("eta_sq");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Eta squared: 0.027");],));
]
]
Only about 3% ($eta^2 approx 0.027$) of the differences in stress between students have to do with their faculty; the rest is about individual student differences. By common guidelines, an $eta^2$ of 0.01 is small, 0.06 medium, and 0.14 large @cohen1988, so this is a real but small effect. For the research question, the answer is that faculty matters, but knowing a student's faculty says very little about how stressed that student is.

=== Post-hoc tests
<post-hoc-tests>
ANOVA says that the faculties differ, but not which ones. #strong[Post-hoc tests] compare every pair while keeping the family-wise error rate at 5%. Tukey's Honest Significant Difference (HSD) test is the standard choice, implemented in #NormalTok("statsmodels");:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" statsmodels.stats.multicomp ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" pairwise_tukeyhsd");],
[],
[#NormalTok("tukey ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pairwise_tukeyhsd(endog");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("study[");#StringTok("\"stress\"");#NormalTok("], groups");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("study[");#StringTok("\"faculty\"");#NormalTok("], alpha");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("0.05");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(tukey)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("          Multiple Comparison of Means - Tukey HSD, FWER=0.05           ");],
[#NormalTok("========================================================================");],
[#NormalTok("     group1           group2      meandiff p-adj   lower   upper  reject");],
[#NormalTok("------------------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("       Education  Health Sciences   0.1943 0.1368 -0.0338  0.4224  False");],
[#NormalTok("       Education       Humanities   -0.143 0.5615 -0.4036  0.1175  False");],
[#NormalTok("       Education Natural Sciences  -0.0588  0.975 -0.3265   0.209  False");],
[#NormalTok("       Education  Social Sciences   0.1222 0.6535 -0.1236  0.3679  False");],
[#NormalTok(" Health Sciences       Humanities  -0.3373 0.0035 -0.5959 -0.0788   True");],
[#NormalTok(" Health Sciences Natural Sciences  -0.2531 0.0707 -0.5189  0.0127  False");],
[#NormalTok(" Health Sciences  Social Sciences  -0.0721 0.9275 -0.3158  0.1715  False");],
[#NormalTok("      Humanities Natural Sciences   0.0843 0.9352 -0.2098  0.3784  False");],
[#NormalTok("      Humanities  Social Sciences   0.2652 0.0636  -0.009  0.5395  False");],
[#NormalTok("Natural Sciences  Social Sciences   0.1809 0.3974 -0.1002   0.462  False");],
[#NormalTok("------------------------------------------------------------------------");],));
]
]
Each row compares two faculties: the difference in average stress (#NormalTok("meandiff");), its 95% confidence interval, an adjusted p-value (#NormalTok("p-adj");), and whether to reject the null hypothesis (#NormalTok("reject");). Only one comparison is significant: Health Sciences students are more stressed than Humanities students, by about 0.34 points on the 1-to-5 scale ($p = 0.004$). All other pair comparisons have adjusted p-values well above 0.05.

=== Assumptions of ANOVA
<assumptions-of-anova>
ANOVA makes the same assumptions as the t-test: independent observations, roughly normal data within each group (or large groups), and similar spread in each group (homoscedasticity). The spread can be checked with #strong[Levene's test] in #NormalTok("scipy.stats");:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("faculty_groups ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" [group[");#StringTok("\"stress\"");#NormalTok("].dropna() ");#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" _, group ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" study.groupby(");#StringTok("\"faculty\"");#NormalTok(")]");],
[#NormalTok("stat_levene, p_levene ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.levene(");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok("faculty_groups)");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Levene's test: F = ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("stat_levene");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok(", p = ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("p_levene");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Levene's test: F = 0.943, p = 0.439");],));
]
]
The p-value is large ($p = 0.439$), so there is no evidence that the spread differs between faculties. When the assumptions fail, the #strong[Kruskal-Wallis test] is the non-parametric alternative, comparing ranks instead of averages:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("stat_kw, p_kw ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.kruskal(");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok("faculty_groups)");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Kruskal-Wallis test: H = ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("stat_kw");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok(", p = ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("p_kw");#SpecialCharTok(":.4f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Kruskal-Wallis test: H = 15.460, p = 0.0038");],));
]
]
It agrees with the ANOVA ($p = 0.004$).

== Two-way ANOVA
<two-way-anova>
Wellbeing might depend on the programme (Master's or PhD), on study mode (full-time or part-time), or on a combination of both. A #strong[two-way ANOVA] examines all three possibilities at once. The #strong[main effect] of programme is the difference between Master's and PhD students on average, and the main effect of study mode is the difference between full-time and part-time students. The #strong[interaction] asks whether the effect of study mode #emph[depends on] the programme: part-time study might, for example, be harder on PhD students than on Master's students.

An #strong[interaction plot] shows the group averages as lines. If the lines are parallel, there is no interaction: the effect of study mode is the same in both programmes.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("wb_summary ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" study.groupby([");#StringTok("\"programme\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"study_mode\"");#NormalTok("])[");#StringTok("\"wellbeing\"");#NormalTok("].mean().reset_index()");],
[],
[#NormalTok("fig, ax ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" plt.subplots(figsize");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.8");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("sns.lineplot(");],
[#NormalTok("    data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("wb_summary,");],
[#NormalTok("    x");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"programme\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    y");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"wellbeing\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    hue");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"study_mode\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    marker");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"o\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    markersize");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("8");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    linewidth");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    palette");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("[");#StringTok("\"#1d588c\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"#b4762e\"");#NormalTok("],");],
[#NormalTok("    ax");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("ax");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_xlabel(");#StringTok("\"\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_ylabel(");#StringTok("\"Average wellbeing\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.legend(title");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"Study mode\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("plt.tight_layout()");],
[#NormalTok("plt.show()");],));
#figure([
#box(image("08-anova-regression_files/figure-typst/fig-interaction-output-1.svg", alt: "Two lines, for full-time and part-time students, across Master's and PhD. Part-time is lower in both programmes; the lines are roughly parallel."))
], caption: figure.caption(
