The output contains three results that matter for the research question. The #strong[coefficients] table gives the estimate of each coefficient, its standard error, a t statistic, and a p-value testing whether it is zero. The slope for #NormalTok("sleep_hours"); is about 0.08: each extra hour of sleep goes with a GPA about 0.08 higher, and the p-value ($p < .001$) shows that this is not chance. #strong[R-squared] ($R^2 = 0.064$) is the share of the variation in GPA that the model explains; here it is about 6%, so sleep explains about 6% of the differences in GPA. Sleep matters, but it is far from the whole story, as the scatter plot suggested. Finally, the scale estimate (square root of MSE) shows the typical residual distance is about 0.32 grade points.

=== Multiple regression
<multiple-regression>
Many things affect grades at once. #strong[Multiple regression] includes several predictors in one model:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gpa_model ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"gpa ~ sleep_hours + study_hours + stress + support\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("study).fit()");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(gpa_model.summary())");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                            OLS Regression Results                            ");],
[#NormalTok("==============================================================================");],
[#NormalTok("Dep. Variable:                    gpa   R-squared:                       0.252");],
[#NormalTok("Model:                            OLS   Adj. R-squared:                  0.247");],
[#NormalTok("Method:                 Least Squares   F-statistic:                     49.14");],
[#NormalTok("Date:                Fri, 23 Oct 2026   Prob (F-statistic):           1.25e-35");],
[#NormalTok("Time:                        23:16:04   Log-Likelihood:                -95.042");],
[#NormalTok("No. Observations:                 587   AIC:                             200.1");],
[#NormalTok("Df Residuals:                     582   BIC:                             222.0");],
[#NormalTok("Df Model:                           4                                         ");],
[#NormalTok("Covariance Type:            nonrobust                                         ");],
[#NormalTok("===============================================================================");],
[#NormalTok("                  coef    std err          t      P>|t|      [0.025      0.975]");],
[#NormalTok("-------------------------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept       2.1374      0.145     14.712      0.000       1.852       2.423");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_hours     0.1060      0.014      7.487      0.000       0.078       0.134");],
[#NormalTok("study_hours     0.0069      0.001      6.270      0.000       0.005       0.009");],
[#NormalTok("stress         -0.0835      0.018     -4.685      0.000      -0.119      -0.049");],
[#NormalTok("support         0.1107      0.017      6.632      0.000       0.078       0.144");],
[#NormalTok("==============================================================================");],
[#NormalTok("Omnibus:                        1.577   Durbin-Watson:                   2.062");],
[#NormalTok("Prob(Omnibus):                  0.455   Jarque-Bera (JB):                1.661");],
[#NormalTok("Skew:                          -0.106   Prob(JB):                        0.436");],
[#NormalTok("Kurtosis:                       2.850   Cond. No.                         389.");],
[#NormalTok("==============================================================================");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Notes:");],
[#NormalTok("[1] Standard Errors assume that the covariance matrix of the errors is correctly specified.");],));
]
]
Each coefficient now means the change in predicted GPA for a one-unit increase in that predictor, #strong[holding the other predictors constant]. Among students with the same study hours, stress, and support, each extra hour of sleep goes with a GPA about 0.11 higher. Each extra hour of study per week adds about 0.007 points, each point of stress lowers GPA by about 0.08 points, and each point of supervisor support raises it by about 0.11 points. All four are significant ($p < .001$).

Together, the four predictors explain about 25% ($R^2 = 0.252$) of the variation in GPA. That may sound modest, but grades depend on many things no survey measures, from ability to luck on exam day. In social and educational research, models that explain a quarter of the variation are common and useful. The #strong[adjusted R-squared] ($0.247$) is slightly lower, because it corrects for the number of predictors: adding any predictor, even a useless one, raises R-squared a little, but only a useful one raises the adjusted version.

The output shows $N = 587$ (from the 600 original students, 13 had missing values in at least one variable). #NormalTok("statsmodels"); automatically drops missing observations from the regression. The number of students each model is based on should always be reported in your thesis.

=== Categorical predictors
<categorical-predictors>
Categorical variables can be predictors too. Gender, for example, can be added to the model to see whether it matters once the other predictors are taken into account:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gpa_gender ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"gpa ~ sleep_hours + study_hours + stress + support + C(gender)\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("study).fit()");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(gpa_gender.summary().tables[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("=====================================================================================");],
[#NormalTok("                        coef    std err          t      P>|t|      [0.025      0.975]");],
[#NormalTok("-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept             2.1237      0.147     14.422      0.000       1.835       2.413");],
[#NormalTok("C(gender)[T.Male]     0.0138      0.024      0.580      0.562      -0.033       0.061");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_hours           0.1066      0.014      7.505      0.000       0.079       0.134");],
[#NormalTok("study_hours           0.0070      0.001      6.292      0.000       0.005       0.009");],
[#NormalTok("stress               -0.0828      0.018     -4.633      0.000      -0.118      -0.048");],
[#NormalTok("support               0.1105      0.017      6.614      0.000       0.078       0.143");],
[#NormalTok("=====================================================================================");],));
]
]
Python turns a categorical predictor into a comparison with a #strong[reference category], by default the first in alphabetical order: here #NormalTok("Female");. The coefficient #NormalTok("C(gender)[T.Male]"); is the difference between men and women, holding everything else constant. It is tiny ($0.014$) and far from significant ($p = 0.56$): there is no evidence that gender affects GPA. That is a finding too, and worth reporting.

For a variable with more categories, such as faculty, #NormalTok("C(faculty)"); compares each category with the reference. A different reference category can be chosen explicitly using #NormalTok("Treatment");:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" patsy.contrasts ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" Treatment");],
[],
[#NormalTok("gpa_fac ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"gpa ~ C(faculty, Treatment(reference='Humanities'))\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("study).fit()");],
