=== Separating an effect from a confounder
<separating-an-effect-from-a-confounder>
Chapters 5 and 6 found that students who take more caffeine have lower GPAs, but also sleep less. Two explanations are possible, and #ref(<fig-confounder>, supplement: [Figure]) draws them. In the first, caffeine itself harms grades. In the second, sleep is a common cause: students who sleep less both drink more coffee and get lower grades, and caffeine has no effect of its own.

#figure([
#block[
#box(image("08-anova-regression_files\\figure-typst\\mermaid-figure-1.png", height: 7.66in, width: 2.7in))

]
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Two explanations for the link between caffeine and GPA. Left: caffeine affects GPA. Right: sleep affects both caffeine and GPA, which creates a link between them even if caffeine has no effect.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-confounder>


Regression can tell the two apart, by comparing students who sleep the same amount. Caffeine alone comes first:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("fit_caf1 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"gpa ~ caffeine_mg\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("study).fit()");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(fit_caf1.summary().tables[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("===============================================================================");],
[#NormalTok("                  coef    std err          t      P>|t|      [0.025      0.975]");],
[#NormalTok("-------------------------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept       3.1849      0.022    145.063      0.000       3.142       3.228");],
[#NormalTok("caffeine_mg    -0.0004   9.24e-05     -4.688      0.000      -0.001      -0.000");],
[#NormalTok("===============================================================================");],));
]
]
Caffeine has a significant negative association with GPA ($p < .001$). Sleep is then added to the model:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("fit_caf2 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"gpa ~ caffeine_mg + sleep_hours\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("study).fit()");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(fit_caf2.summary().tables[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("===============================================================================");],
[#NormalTok("                  coef    std err          t      P>|t|      [0.025      0.975]");],
[#NormalTok("-------------------------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept       2.6381      0.124     21.304      0.000       2.395       2.881");],
[#NormalTok("caffeine_mg -7.807e-05      0.000     -0.654      0.514      -0.000       0.000");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_hours     0.0739      0.017      4.458      0.000       0.041       0.106");],
[#NormalTok("===============================================================================");],));
]
]
Once sleep is held constant, caffeine's coefficient shrinks from $- 0.0004$ to almost zero ($- 0.00008$) and is no longer significant ($p = 0.51$), while sleep stays strongly significant ($p < .001$). Among students who sleep the same amount, caffeine makes no difference to grades. Caffeine looked harmful only because heavy caffeine users sleep less: sleep was a #strong[confounder], and the data supports the second explanation.

The general rule is that a confounder is a common cause of the predictor and the outcome. Adjusting for it in a regression compares like with like, students who are the same on the confounder, and removes the misleading part of the association.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
Regression can remove the effect of a confounder only if you measured it and put it in the model. It cannot adjust for anything you did not measure. That is why, outside a randomised experiment like the workshop, regression shows associations #emph[adjusted for] the variables in the model, not proof of cause.

]
, 
title: 
[
Regression adjusts only for what you include
]
, 
background_color: 
rgb("#f7dddc")
, 
icon_color: 
rgb("#CC1914")
, 
icon: 
fa-exclamation()
, 
body_background_color: 
white
)
]
=== Curved relationships
<curved-relationships>
A straight line assumes that every extra hour of study adds the same amount to GPA, which is hard to believe: the difference between 5 and 15 hours a week is probably larger than the difference between 45 and 55. Adding a squared term lets the line curve:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gpa_curve ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"gpa ~ study_hours + np.power(study_hours, 2)\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("study).fit()");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(gpa_curve.summary().tables[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("============================================================================================");],
[#NormalTok("                               coef    std err          t      P>|t|      [0.025      0.975]");],
[#NormalTok("--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept                    2.8630      0.054     53.211      0.000       2.757       2.969");],
[#NormalTok("study_hours                  0.0183      0.004      4.687      0.000       0.011       0.026");],
[#NormalTok("np.power(study_hours, 2)    -0.0003   6.49e-05     -4.430      0.000      -0.000      -0.000");],
[#NormalTok("============================================================================================");],));
]
]
In the formula, #NormalTok("np.power(study_hours, 2)"); calculates the squared term. The squared term is negative and significant: the line bends downwards. #ref(<fig-curve>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the shape.

