== Logistic regression
<logistic-regression>
The third research question concerns a yes-or-no outcome: whether a student has considered dropping out. Linear regression is the wrong tool here, because it could predict probabilities below 0 or above 1, which make no sense. #strong[Logistic regression] models the probability of a "yes" in a way that always stays between 0 and 1.

=== Odds and odds ratios
<odds-and-odds-ratios>
Logistic regression works with #strong[odds] rather than probabilities. The odds of an event are the probability that it happens divided by the probability that it does not. The cross-tabulation from Chapter 7 shows how they are calculated:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("jobs ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.crosstab(students[");#StringTok("\"employment\"");#NormalTok("], students[");#StringTok("\"considering_dropout\"");#NormalTok("])");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(jobs)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("considering_dropout   No  Yes");],
[#NormalTok("employment                   ");],
[#NormalTok("Full-time job         72   25");],
[#NormalTok("None                 265   42");],
[#NormalTok("Part-time job        173   23");],));
]
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("odds_full ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" jobs.loc[");#StringTok("\"Full-time job\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" jobs.loc[");#StringTok("\"Full-time job\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("odds_none ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" jobs.loc[");#StringTok("\"None\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" jobs.loc[");#StringTok("\"None\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("odds_ratio ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" odds_full ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" odds_none");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Full-time odds: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("odds_full");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"No job odds:    ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("odds_none");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Odds ratio:     ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("odds_ratio");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Full-time odds: 0.35");],
[#NormalTok("No job odds:    0.16");],
[#NormalTok("Odds ratio:     2.19");],));
]
]
Among students with a full-time job, 19 have considered dropping out and 57 have not, which gives odds of about 0.33. Among students without a job, the odds are 0.15. The #strong[odds ratio] compares the two: students with a full-time job have about 2.2 times the odds of considering dropout. An odds ratio of 1 means no difference, above 1 means higher odds, and below 1 means lower odds.

=== A model for dropout
<a-model-for-dropout>
In Python, #NormalTok("statsmodels.formula.api.logit"); fits logistic regression. The outcome must be coded 0 and 1:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("study[");#StringTok("\"dropout\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" (study[");#StringTok("\"considering_dropout\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok(").astype(");#BuiltInTok("int");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("dropout_model ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.logit(");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"dropout ~ stress + support + financial_worry + C(employment) + C(study_mode)\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("study");],
[#NormalTok(").fit(disp");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("False");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(dropout_model.summary())");],));
