The coefficients are on the scale of #strong[log-odds], which is hard to interpret directly. Taking the exponential (#NormalTok("np.exp");) turns them into odds ratios and confidence intervals:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("odds_table ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.DataFrame({");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"odds_ratio\"");#NormalTok(": np.exp(dropout_model.params),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"ci_lower\"");#NormalTok(": np.exp(dropout_model.conf_int()[");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok("]),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"ci_upper\"");#NormalTok(": np.exp(dropout_model.conf_int()[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("}).");#BuiltInTok("round");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(odds_table)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                                odds_ratio  ci_lower  ci_upper");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept                             0.02      0.00      0.20");],
[#NormalTok("C(employment)[T.None]                 0.55      0.24      1.31");],
[#NormalTok("C(employment)[T.Part-time job]        0.58      0.24      1.42");],
[#NormalTok("C(study_mode)[T.Part-time]            1.32      0.64      2.71");],
[#NormalTok("stress                                3.60      2.25      5.77");],
[#NormalTok("support                               0.33      0.22      0.49");],
[#NormalTok("financial_worry                       1.57      1.24      2.00");],));
]
]
Holding the other predictors constant, each extra point of #strong[stress] multiplies the odds of considering dropout by about 3.6 (OR = 3.60, 95% CI \[2.25, 5.77\]), and each extra point of #strong[financial worry] by about 1.6 (OR = 1.57, 95% CI \[1.24, 2.00\]). Each extra point of #strong[supervisor support] multiplies them by about 0.33 (95% CI \[0.22, 0.49\]), which cuts them by about 67%. The hypothesis stated in Chapter 5, that higher stress raises the odds of considering dropout, is strongly supported: the confidence interval for stress lies entirely above 1.

Something interesting has happened to #strong[employment]. In Chapter 7, and in the unadjusted odds ratio above, students with full-time jobs had about twice the odds of considering dropping out. In this model, the confidence interval for employment includes 1 (comparing None vs Full-time, OR = 0.55, 95% CI \[0.24, 1.31\]; Part-time vs Full-time, OR = 0.58, 95% CI \[0.24, 1.42\]): once stress and financial worry are taken into account, having a job adds little on its own. The likely explanation is that a full-time job matters #emph[because] of the stress and money pressure that come with it. This is the kind of insight multiple regression makes possible.

=== Predicted probabilities
<predicted-probabilities>
Odds ratios are hard for many readers; predicted probabilities are easier. The model can predict the probability of considering dropout for two example students who are identical except for their stress and support:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("examples ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.DataFrame({");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"stress\"");#NormalTok(":          [");#FloatTok("2.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.0");#NormalTok("],");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"support\"");#NormalTok(":         [");#FloatTok("4.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.0");#NormalTok("],");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"financial_worry\"");#NormalTok(": [");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok("],");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"employment\"");#NormalTok(":      [");#StringTok("\"None\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"None\"");#NormalTok("],");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"study_mode\"");#NormalTok(":      [");#StringTok("\"Full-time\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Full-time\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("})");],
[#NormalTok("predicted_probs ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" dropout_model.predict(examples)");],
[#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" i, prob ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("enumerate");#NormalTok("(predicted_probs, ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("):");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Student ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("i");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok(": ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("prob ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("100");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok("% chance of considering dropout\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Student 1: 1.1% chance of considering dropout");],
[#NormalTok("Student 2: 41.6% chance of considering dropout");],));
]
]
A student with low stress and good support has about a 1% chance of considering dropout; a stressed student with little support, about a 42% chance. For a thesis's recommendations, that contrast is more persuasive than any coefficient.

Whether the model can #emph[predict] who will consider dropping out, and how that should be tested fairly, is a different question, about prediction rather than explanation. It is the subject of Chapters 11 and 12.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
The classic #NormalTok("trees"); dataset records the girth, height, and timber volume of 31 felled black cherry trees. Foresters need to estimate a standing tree's volume from measurements they can take without cutting it down, a classic job for multiple regression:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tree_model ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"Volume ~ Girth + Height\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("trees).fit()");],
