== Constructs and latent variables
<constructs-and-latent-variables>
A single questionnaire item is a poor measure of a construct. The answer to "I feel unable to control important things in my studies" depends on the student's stress, but also on how they read the question that day, on their mood, and on how they use the answer scale. Each item therefore carries two things: a signal from the construct, and noise of its own. A #strong[latent variable] is the construct thought to lie behind the items: it is not observed, but it is assumed to cause part of every answer.

Averaging several items keeps the signal, which all items share, and lets the noise, which differs from item to item, partly cancel out. A simulation shows how much this helps. It creates a "true" stress level for 600 imaginary students, then six items, each equal to the true level plus its own random noise, and compares how closely one item and the average of several items follow the true level:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("np.random.seed(");#DecValTok("42");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("true_stress ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.random.normal(");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("600");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("simulated_items ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.column_stack([true_stress ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" np.random.normal(");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("600");#NormalTok(") ");#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" _ ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(")])");],
[],
[#NormalTok("corrs ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" {}");],
[#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" k ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok("):");],
[#NormalTok("    mean_k ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" simulated_items[:, :k].mean(axis");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    r ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("float");#NormalTok("(np.corrcoef(mean_k, true_stress)[");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("    corrs[");#SpecialStringTok("f\"");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("k");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok(" items\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("round");#NormalTok("(r, ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" label, val ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" corrs.items():");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("label");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok(": r = ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("val");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("1 items: r = 0.68");],
[#NormalTok("2 items: r = 0.8");],
[#NormalTok("3 items: r = 0.85");],
[#NormalTok("4 items: r = 0.88");],
[#NormalTok("5 items: r = 0.9");],
[#NormalTok("6 items: r = 0.92");],));
]
]
In this simulation, one item correlates only about 0.72 with the true stress level, while the average of six items correlates about 0.93. This is why questionnaires use several items for each construct, and why the items are combined into a scale score (Chapter 3).

The approach rests on an assumption that must be checked: that the items of a scale really reflect one common construct, and not several. If some stress items actually measured tiredness, averaging them would mix two things. Chapter 5 called this #strong[construct validity]. The methods in this chapter provide evidence for it: factor analysis shows whether the items group as intended, and Cronbach's alpha shows whether the items of each group agree with each other, which Chapter 5 called #strong[internal consistency].

