Only 396 of the 600 students answered all 22 items: a small share of missing answers per item adds up to many incomplete students. Factor analysis, below, can use the incomplete students too.

The table shows how much of the total variation each component captures. The first component alone captures 30%, and the first four together capture 56%.

=== The number of components
<the-number-of-components>
No single rule decides how many components to keep, so researchers use several. The #strong[Kaiser rule] keeps components whose #strong[eigenvalue], the amount of variation they capture, is greater than 1, that is, more than one original variable's worth; here, 4 components pass. The #strong[scree plot] shows the eigenvalues in order, and the researcher looks for the "elbow" where the line flattens out. #strong[Parallel analysis] compares the eigenvalues with those from random data of the same size, and keeps the components that beat random data; it is the most reliable of the three.

We can draw the scree plot directly with Matplotlib and Seaborn:

