The table shows four clear factors. Each collects the items of one scale: the support items on one, the stress items on another, the burnout items on a third, and the satisfaction items on the fourth. The reversed item, #NormalTok("stress_4"); ("I feel confident handling problems in my studies"), loads with the opposite sign (+0.52 against −0.51 to −0.69 for the other stress items), which is exactly right for a reversed item: agreeing with it means less stress, and it is why the item must be reversed before the stress score is calculated (Chapter 3). One item, #NormalTok("burnout_3"); ("Deadlines make me feel overwhelmed"), loads substantially on both burnout (−0.56) and stress (−0.46). Reading the item, that makes sense: feeling overwhelmed by deadlines is both. Such a #strong[cross-loading] item is worth discussing in a thesis, and sometimes worth rewording or removing in future studies.

=== Reliability and Cronbach's alpha
<reliability-and-cronbachs-alpha>
Factor analysis shows that the items of each scale belong together. #strong[Reliability] asks a related question: whether the items of a scale give consistent results (Chapter 5). The most common measure of this internal consistency is #strong[Cronbach's alpha], which ranges from 0 to 1:

$ alpha = frac(k, k - 1) (1 - frac(sum s_i^2, s_(upright("total"))^2)) $

It rises when the items correlate strongly with each other, and also when there are more items, for the reason shown by the simulation at the start of the chapter. As a rough guide, 0.7 or above is acceptable and 0.8 or above good for research use:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#KeywordTok("def");#NormalTok(" cronbach_alpha(df: pd.DataFrame) ");#OperatorTok("->");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("float");#NormalTok(":");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#CommentTok("\"\"\"Calculate Cronbach's alpha for internal consistency.\"\"\"");],
[#NormalTok("    df_clean ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" df.dropna()");],
[#NormalTok("    k ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" df_clean.shape[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("    item_variances ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" df_clean.var(ddof");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(").");#BuiltInTok("sum");#NormalTok("()");],
[#NormalTok("    total_variance ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" df_clean.");#BuiltInTok("sum");#NormalTok("(axis");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(").var(ddof");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#ControlFlowTok("return");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("float");#NormalTok("((k ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" (k ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")) ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" (item_variances ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" total_variance)))");],
[],
