=== Hierarchical clustering
<hierarchical-clustering>
#strong[Hierarchical clustering] takes a different approach. It starts with every student as their own cluster and repeatedly merges the two most similar clusters, until everyone is in one. The result is a tree, called a #strong[dendrogram], which can be cut at any height to give any number of clusters, without choosing $k$ in advance. Ward's method merges clusters so that they stay as compact as possible:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Z ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" linkage(profile_scaled, method");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"ward\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("fig, ax ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" plt.subplots(figsize");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("7.5");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("dendrogram(");],
[#NormalTok("    Z,");],
[#NormalTok("    truncate_mode");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"lastp\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    p");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("25");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    show_leaf_counts");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("True");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    color_threshold");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("Z[");#OperatorTok("-");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("],");],
[#NormalTok("    ax");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("ax");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_xlabel(");#StringTok("\"Cluster branches (truncated)\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_ylabel(");#StringTok("\"Ward Distance\"");#NormalTok(")");],
