Two things are clear. Almost everyone gets slower as the days go by. And people differ: some start faster than others (different #strong[intercepts]), and some slow down more than others (different #strong[slopes]).

=== Random intercepts
<random-intercepts>
An ordinary regression would fit one line through all 180 points, as if they came from 180 different people. A #strong[random intercept model] fits one average line, but lets each person have their own starting level. In Python, #NormalTok("smf.mixedlm()"); from #NormalTok("statsmodels.formula.api"); fits linear mixed models. The argument #NormalTok("groups=sleepstudy[\"Subject\"]"); assigns each person their own random intercept:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("sleep_ri ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.mixedlm(");#StringTok("\"Reaction ~ Days\"");#NormalTok(", sleepstudy, groups");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("sleepstudy[");#StringTok("\"Subject\"");#NormalTok("]).fit()");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(sleep_ri.summary())");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("         Mixed Linear Model Regression Results");],
[#NormalTok("========================================================");],
[#NormalTok("Model:             MixedLM Dependent Variable: Reaction ");],
[#NormalTok("No. Observations:  180     Method:             REML     ");],
[#NormalTok("No. Groups:        18      Scale:              960.4568 ");],
[#NormalTok("Min. group size:   10      Log-Likelihood:     -893.2325");],
[#NormalTok("Max. group size:   10      Converged:          Yes      ");],
[#NormalTok("Mean group size:   10.0                                 ");],
[#NormalTok("--------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("           Coef.   Std.Err.   z    P>|z|  [0.025  0.975]");],
[#NormalTok("--------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept  251.405    9.747 25.794 0.000 232.302 270.508");],
[#NormalTok("Days        10.467    0.804 13.015 0.000   8.891  12.044");],
[#NormalTok("Group Var 1378.176   17.156                             ");],
[#NormalTok("========================================================");],));
]
]
The output has two important parts. The #strong[fixed effects] describe the average line, as in ordinary regression: on day 0, the average reaction time is about 251 ms, and each day of sleep restriction adds about 10.5 ms. The #strong[random effects] describe how much people vary around the average line. The standard deviation of the intercepts, about 37 ms ($sqrt(upright("Group Var")) = sqrt(1378.18)$), says that people's starting levels typically differ from the average by that much; the residual standard deviation, about 31 ms ($sqrt(upright("Scale")) = sqrt(960.46)$), is the day-to-day variation within a person.

=== The intraclass correlation
<the-intraclass-correlation>
The random intercept model splits the variation in reaction times into two parts: stable differences between people, and variation within each person from day to day. The #strong[intraclass correlation] (ICC) compares them:

$ upright("ICC") = frac(sigma_(upright("between"))^2, sigma_(upright("between"))^2 + sigma_(upright("within"))^2) $

In #NormalTok("statsmodels");, the between-group variance is in #NormalTok("cov_re"); and the residual variance is in #NormalTok("scale");:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("var_between ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sleep_ri.cov_re.iloc[");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("var_within ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sleep_ri.scale");],
[#NormalTok("icc_sleep ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" var_between ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" (var_between ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" var_within)");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"ICC: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("icc_sleep");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("ICC: 0.589");],));
]
]
About 59% of the variation in reaction times is due to stable differences between people. The ICC can be read as a comparison of how much people differ from each other with how much each person varies. An ICC of 0 would mean the grouping does not matter: two measurements of the same person are no more alike than measurements of two different people. The higher the ICC, the more the measurements of one person repeat each other, and the more wrong an ordinary regression would be.

=== Random slopes
<random-slopes>
The random intercept model still assumes that everyone slows down at the same rate, which #ref(<fig-sleepstudy>, supplement: [Figure]) shows is not true. A #strong[random slope model] lets each person have their own slope as well. Specifying #NormalTok("re_formula=\"~Days\""); allows the effect of #NormalTok("Days"); to vary across subjects:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("sleep_rs ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.mixedlm(");#StringTok("\"Reaction ~ Days\"");#NormalTok(", sleepstudy, groups");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("sleepstudy[");#StringTok("\"Subject\"");#NormalTok("], re_formula");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"~Days\"");#NormalTok(").fit()");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(sleep_rs.summary())");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("            Mixed Linear Model Regression Results");],
[#NormalTok("==============================================================");],
[#NormalTok("Model:               MixedLM   Dependent Variable:   Reaction ");],
[#NormalTok("No. Observations:    180       Method:               REML     ");],
[#NormalTok("No. Groups:          18        Scale:                654.9405 ");],
[#NormalTok("Min. group size:     10        Log-Likelihood:       -871.8141");],
[#NormalTok("Max. group size:     10        Converged:            Yes      ");],
[#NormalTok("Mean group size:     10.0                                     ");],
[#NormalTok("--------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("                  Coef.  Std.Err.   z    P>|z|  [0.025  0.975]");],
[#NormalTok("--------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept        251.405    6.825 36.838 0.000 238.029 264.781");],
[#NormalTok("Days              10.467    1.546  6.771 0.000   7.438  13.497");],
[#NormalTok("Group Var        612.096   11.881                             ");],
[#NormalTok("Group x Days Cov   9.605    1.821                             ");],
[#NormalTok("Days Var          35.072    0.610                             ");],
[#NormalTok("==============================================================");],));
]
]
The average effect of a day of sleep restriction is the same, about 10.5 ms, but its standard error has grown from 0.80 to 1.55. People's slopes vary with a standard deviation of about 5.9 ms per day ($sqrt(35.07)$), so for most people a day of sleep restriction adds somewhere between about 5 and 16 ms. Ignoring that variation made the random intercept model too confident about the average.

A #strong[likelihood ratio test] compares the fit of the two models:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("lr_stat ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("-");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" (sleep_ri.llf ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" sleep_rs.llf)");],
[#NormalTok("p_val ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.chi2.sf(lr_stat, df");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Likelihood Ratio Chi2(2): ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("lr_stat");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok(", p-value: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("p_val");#SpecialCharTok(":.4e}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"AIC - Random Intercept: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sleep_ri");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("aic");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok(", Random Slope: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sleep_rs");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("aic");#SpecialCharTok(":.1f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Likelihood Ratio Chi2(2): 42.84, p-value: 4.9900e-10");],
[#NormalTok("AIC - Random Intercept: nan, Random Slope: nan");],));
]
]
The p-value is tiny ($p < 0.001$): the random slopes improve the model clearly. The #strong[AIC] agrees: lower is better, and the random slope model has a substantially lower AIC (1755.6 vs 1794.5).

== Change in wellbeing over two years
<change-in-wellbeing-over-two-years>
The wellbeing study has up to four semester records for each student, joined with their background information. The variable #NormalTok("time"); counts semesters from the first, so that time 0 is semester 1:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("panel ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" (");],
[#NormalTok("    semesters");],
[#NormalTok("    .merge(students, on");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"student_id\"");#NormalTok(")");],
