The 2326 semester records carry about as much information about average wellbeing as 716 independent observations would. Treating them as 2326 independent records would therefore be seriously wrong.

=== Why ordinary regression misleads
<why-ordinary-regression-misleads>
The first question about change is whether wellbeing declines over time. The #emph[wrong] way to answer it is an ordinary regression that ignores the structure:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("ols_fit ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.ols(");#StringTok("\"wellbeing ~ time\"");#NormalTok(", data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("panel).fit()");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(ols_fit.summary().tables[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("==============================================================================");],
[#NormalTok("                 coef    std err          t      P>|t|      [0.025      0.975]");],
[#NormalTok("------------------------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept     61.6320      0.412    149.424      0.000      60.823      62.441");],
[#NormalTok("time          -0.3795      0.224     -1.698      0.090      -0.818       0.059");],
[#NormalTok("==============================================================================");],));
]
]
The slope is slightly negative, but not significant ($p = 0.548$). The mixed model, with a random intercept and slope for each student, gives a different result:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("wb_growth ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.mixedlm(");#StringTok("\"wellbeing ~ time\"");#NormalTok(", panel, groups");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("panel[");#StringTok("\"student_id\"");#NormalTok("], re_formula");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"~time\"");#NormalTok(").fit()");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(wb_growth.summary())");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("C:\\Users\\polla\\Drives\\Locals\\pytorch\\venv\\Lib\\site-packages\\statsmodels\\regression\\mixed_linear_model.py:2384: ConvergenceWarning: Maximum Likelihood optimization failed to converge. Check mle_retvals");],
[#NormalTok("  rslt = super().fit(");],
[#NormalTok("C:\\Users\\polla\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\ipykernel_22420\\3258426107.py:1: ConvergenceWarning: Retrying MixedLM optimization with lbfgs");],
[#NormalTok("  wb_growth = smf.mixedlm(\"wellbeing ~ time\", panel, groups=panel[\"student_id\"], re_formula=\"~time\").fit()");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("            Mixed Linear Model Regression Results");],
[#NormalTok("=============================================================");],
[#NormalTok("Model:               MixedLM  Dependent Variable:  wellbeing ");],
[#NormalTok("No. Observations:    2326     Method:              REML      ");],
[#NormalTok("No. Groups:          600      Scale:               30.4037   ");],
[#NormalTok("Min. group size:     2        Log-Likelihood:      -8122.8912");],
[#NormalTok("Max. group size:     4        Converged:           Yes       ");],
[#NormalTok("Mean group size:     3.9                                     ");],
[#NormalTok("-------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("                  Coef.  Std.Err.    z    P>|z| [0.025 0.975]");],
[#NormalTok("-------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept         61.646    0.475 129.646 0.000 60.714 62.578");],
[#NormalTok("time              -0.435    0.110  -3.971 0.000 -0.650 -0.220");],
[#NormalTok("Group Var        114.264    1.745                            ");],
[#NormalTok("Group x time Cov  -0.855    0.252                            ");],
[#NormalTok("time Var           0.742    0.088                            ");],
[#NormalTok("=============================================================");],));
]
]
The average slope is similar, about -0.43 points per semester, but its standard error has fallen from 0.22 to 0.11, and the decline is now clearly significant (\$z = {python} f\"{time\_z:.1f}\"\$, $p < 0.001$). The reason is that the ordinary regression lumps the large, stable differences #emph[between] students into its error term, which drowns the small change #emph[within] each student. The mixed model separates the two, and sees the change clearly.

In the simulation at the start of the chapter, ignoring the structure made a non-existent effect look significant; in the sleep study, ignoring the differences in slopes made the average look more certain than it was; here, ignoring the structure makes a real decline look uncertain. The direction of the error depends on the data, but the error is always there.

=== Confidence intervals and p-values
<confidence-intervals-and-p-values>
In #NormalTok("statsmodels");, linear mixed models report Wald z-statistics, p-values, and 95% confidence intervals directly in the summary table:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("ci_table ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" wb_growth.conf_int()");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(ci_table.loc[[");#StringTok("\"Intercept\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"time\"");#NormalTok("]])");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                   0          1");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept  60.713915  62.577820");],
[#NormalTok("time       -0.649696  -0.220291");],));
]
]
The 95% confidence interval for #NormalTok("time"); (\[-0.65, -0.22\]) lies entirely below zero: wellbeing declines steadily over time. Chapter 5's hypothesis for RQ8, that wellbeing declines over the two years, is supported.

