Notice that the variance for #NormalTok("time"); in the random effects is effectively zero (0.000), and #NormalTok("statsmodels"); warns that the estimate lies on the boundary of the parameter space. This is Python's counterpart to R's "singular fit" warning, indicating that students' GPA trajectories hardly vary in slope over time. When a random slope has near-zero variance, the model is unnecessarily complex, and the standard advice is to simplify by retaining only the random intercept:

