About 13% of all the variation in wellbeing lies between supervisors: students of the same supervisor are noticeably more alike. Most of the variation, 65%%, lies between students within the same supervisor, and the remaining 22%% is change within students from semester to semester. For the university, the supervisor share is a practical finding: who supervises a student makes a measurable difference to their wellbeing. It also means that any comparison of supervisors, or of anything assigned to supervisors, must allow for this grouping, as the simulation at the start of the chapter showed.

== Counts and yes-or-no outcomes
<counts-and-yes-or-no-outcomes>
Like ordinary regression, mixed models extend to other kinds of outcome. When observations are clustered and the outcome is a count (such as the number of supervisor meetings per semester), #strong[Generalized Estimating Equations] (#NormalTok("smf.gee"); with #NormalTok("family=sm.families.Poisson()");) or generalized mixed models account for within-student clustering.

The model below examines whether students who feel more supported meet their supervisor more often. Support is calculated from the questionnaire:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("support_scores ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.DataFrame({");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"student_id\"");#NormalTok(": questionnaire[");#StringTok("\"student_id\"");#NormalTok("],");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"support\"");#NormalTok(": questionnaire.");#BuiltInTok("filter");#NormalTok("(like");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"support_\"");#NormalTok(").mean(axis");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("})");],
[],
[#NormalTok("panel_meetings ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" panel.merge(support_scores, on");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"student_id\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("meetings_gee ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" smf.gee(");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"supervisor_meetings ~ support + C(programme)\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    data");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("panel_meetings,");],
[#NormalTok("    groups");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("panel_meetings[");#StringTok("\"student_id\"");#NormalTok("],");],
[#NormalTok("    family");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("sm.families.Poisson()");],
[#NormalTok(").fit()");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(meetings_gee.summary().tables[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("=======================================================================================");],
[#NormalTok("                          coef    std err          z      P>|z|      [0.025      0.975]");],
[#NormalTok("---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept               0.0446      0.075      0.592      0.554      -0.103       0.192");],
[#NormalTok("C(programme)[T.PhD]     0.2054      0.034      5.977      0.000       0.138       0.273");],
[#NormalTok("support                 0.4018      0.021     18.852      0.000       0.360       0.444");],
[#NormalTok("=======================================================================================");],));
]
]
Poisson coefficients are on a log scale, and #NormalTok("np.exp()"); turns them into #strong[rate ratios]:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("rate_ratios ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.exp(meetings_gee.params)");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Rate Ratios (exp(beta)):\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(rate_ratios.");#BuiltInTok("round");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Rate Ratios (exp(beta)):");],
[#NormalTok("Intercept              1.046");],
[#NormalTok("C(programme)[T.PhD]    1.228");],
[#NormalTok("support                1.494");],
[#NormalTok("dtype: float64");],));
]
]
Each extra point of support goes with about 49% more meetings per semester (\$\\text{RR} = {python} f\"{rr\_support:.2f}\"\$), and PhD students have about 23% more meetings than Master's students (\$\\text{RR} = {python} f\"{rr\_phd:.2f}\"\$), holding support constant. As always with observational data, the direction is not certain: more meetings may also make students feel more supported.

