#part[Machine Learning with Python]
= Introduction to Machine Learning in Python
<sec-ch11>
Some research questions are not about why something happens, but about what will happen. A hospital wants to know which patients are likely to be readmitted, a bank which loans are likely to fail, a university which students are likely to struggle. The goal is not to understand the causes, although that may help, but to make accurate predictions for new cases, early enough to act on them. Such questions need a different standard of evidence. For explanation, a model is judged by whether its coefficients are well estimated and make sense. For prediction, a model is judged by one thing only: how well it works on cases it has never seen.

#strong[Machine learning] is the set of methods and practices for building predictive models and testing them fairly. This chapter introduces its core ideas: the difference between prediction and explanation, why a model must be tested on new data, how models overfit, and how cross-validation and tuning choose a model honestly. It uses Python's industry-standard #strong[scikit-learn] framework, whose components are best understood as the design of a fair test. In the story, Chapter 8 used logistic regression to explain who considers dropping out; Elaf's supervisor now asks whether the university could identify, at the end of a student's first semester, the students at risk, so that help could be offered in time (RQ9).

