<fig-bias-variance>


The training error falls steadily as the model becomes more flexible: a more flexible model can always fit its own data more closely. The error on new data falls at first, reaches its minimum at degree np.int64(3), and then rises. This U shape is known as the #strong[bias-variance trade-off]. A model that is too simple has high #strong[bias]: it misses part of the real pattern, whatever data it is given. A model that is too flexible has high #strong[variance]: it changes a great deal from one sample to another, because it follows each sample's noise. The best predictions come from a model in between, and the only way to find it is to measure performance on data the model did not learn from. The rest of the chapter builds that measurement into every step.

== Designing a fair test
<designing-a-fair-test>
The #strong[scikit-learn] framework provides a principled, modular design that makes a fair test of a predictive model clean and reliable. Each module handles one part of the pipeline: #NormalTok("train_test_split"); partitions the data, #NormalTok("ColumnTransformer"); and pipelines prepare it, estimators specify models, #NormalTok("GridSearchCV"); tunes them, and #NormalTok("metrics"); assesses performance on unseen data.

#ref(<fig-pipeline>, supplement: [Figure]) shows how the pieces fit together: the test data is set aside before anything else happens, all choices are made with the training data alone, and the test data is used once, at the end. The rest of the chapter works through each step.

