Only about 15% of students answer "Yes". Such an #strong[imbalanced] outcome is common in real prediction problems, from rare diseases to fraud, and, as the evaluation below shows, it catches out anyone who judges a model by accuracy alone.

== Splitting the data
<splitting-the-data>
The most important rule of machine learning follows from the simulation above: #strong[never judge a model on the data it learned from.] The first step is therefore to split the data. The #strong[training set] is used to build and tune the model. The #strong[test set] is locked away and used only once, at the very end, to estimate how the final model will perform on new students. The function #NormalTok("train_test_split()"); with #NormalTok("stratify=y"); makes sure that both sets have the same share of "Yes" answers:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("X ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" dropout_data.drop(columns");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("[");#StringTok("\"considering_dropout\"");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("y ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" (dropout_data[");#StringTok("\"considering_dropout\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok(").astype(");#BuiltInTok("int");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" train_test_split(");],
[#NormalTok("    X, y, test_size");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("0.25");#NormalTok(", stratify");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("y, random_state");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2026");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Training observations: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#BuiltInTok("len");#NormalTok("(X_train)");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Testing observations:  ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#BuiltInTok("len");#NormalTok("(X_test)");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Training observations: 450");],
[#NormalTok("Testing observations:  150");],));
]
]
Three quarters of the students are used for training and a quarter are set aside for the final test.

== Preparing the data
<preparing-the-data>
Most models need the data prepared first: missing values filled in, categories turned into numbers, and numeric predictors put on a common scale. A #NormalTok("ColumnTransformer"); bundles these steps cleanly:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("num_cols ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" X.select_dtypes(include");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("[np.number]).columns.tolist()");],
[#NormalTok("cat_cols ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" X.select_dtypes(exclude");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("[np.number]).columns.tolist()");],
[],
[#NormalTok("preprocessor ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ColumnTransformer([");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("\"num\"");#NormalTok(", Pipeline([");],
[#NormalTok("        (");#StringTok("\"imputer\"");#NormalTok(", SimpleImputer(strategy");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"median\"");#NormalTok(")),");],
[#NormalTok("        (");#StringTok("\"scaler\"");#NormalTok(", StandardScaler())");],
[#NormalTok("    ]), num_cols),");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("\"cat\"");#NormalTok(", Pipeline([");],
[#NormalTok("        (");#StringTok("\"imputer\"");#NormalTok(", SimpleImputer(strategy");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"most_frequent\"");#NormalTok(")),");],
[#NormalTok("        (");#StringTok("\"ohe\"");#NormalTok(", OneHotEncoder(drop");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"first\"");#NormalTok(", sparse_output");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("False");#NormalTok(", handle_unknown");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"ignore\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("    ]), cat_cols)");],
[#NormalTok("])");],));
]
The numeric pipeline replaces missing values with the variable's median (#NormalTok("SimpleImputer(strategy=\"median\")");) and standardizes predictors to z-scores (#NormalTok("StandardScaler()");). The categorical pipeline imputes missing categories with the most frequent value and creates one-hot dummy variables (#NormalTok("OneHotEncoder(drop=\"first\")");), dropping the reference level just as linear regression did in Chapter 8.

To see what the preprocessor produces, #NormalTok("fit_transform()"); estimates the parameters from the training set and transforms it:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("X_train_prep ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" preprocessor.fit_transform(X_train)");],
[#NormalTok("feature_names ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" preprocessor.get_feature_names_out()");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Transformed training shape:\"");#NormalTok(", X_train_prep.shape)");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"First 8 prepared feature names:\"");#NormalTok(", feature_names[:");#DecValTok("8");#NormalTok("].tolist())");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Transformed training shape: (450, 24)");],
[#NormalTok("First 8 prepared feature names: ['num__age', 'num__financial_worry', 'num__stress', 'num__burnout', 'num__support', 'num__satisfaction', 'num__gpa', 'num__sleep_hours']");],));
]
]
