[],
[#NormalTok("test_results ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.DataFrame({");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"truth\"");#NormalTok(": y_test.values,");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"pred_class\"");#NormalTok(": y_pred_class,");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"pred_prob\"");#NormalTok(": y_pred_prob");],
[#NormalTok("})");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(test_results.head())");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("   truth  pred_class  pred_prob");],
[#NormalTok("0      0           0   0.031820");],
[#NormalTok("1      1           1   0.644133");],
[#NormalTok("2      0           0   0.177301");],
[#NormalTok("3      0           0   0.042008");],
[#NormalTok("4      0           0   0.030207");],));
]
]
The simplest measure is #strong[accuracy]: the share of students classified correctly:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("acc ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred_class)");],
[#NormalTok("always_no_acc ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" (y_test ");#OperatorTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(").mean()");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Model test accuracy:          ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("acc");#SpecialCharTok(":.1%}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Always predicting 'No' base: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("always_no_acc");#SpecialCharTok(":.1%}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Model test accuracy:          88.0%");],
[#NormalTok("Always predicting 'No' base: 84.7%");],));
]
]
An accuracy of 88.0% sounds good. But a "model" that ignores every predictor and says "No" for every student would be right 84.7% of the time, because most students answer "No", while finding not one of the students at risk. For an imbalanced outcome, accuracy is a misleading measure.

A better measure is the #strong[ROC AUC] (the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve). It is the probability that, of one student who considered dropping out and one who did not, the model gives the first a higher predicted probability than the second. An AUC of 0.5 is no better than a coin toss, and 1.0 is perfect. Unlike accuracy, it is not fooled by an imbalanced outcome:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("auc_test ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" roc_auc_score(y_test, y_pred_prob)");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Test ROC AUC: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("auc_test");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Test ROC AUC: 0.843");],));
]
]
An AUC of 0.84 means the model ranks an at-risk student above a not-at-risk student about 84% of the time: a useful model. Chapter 12 looks at classification measures in much more detail, including how to choose the threshold at which a student is flagged.

== Overfitting in practice
<overfitting-in-practice>
The simulation at the start of the chapter showed overfitting with a flexible curve. The same happens with real data. A dramatic example is #strong[k-nearest neighbours] (k-NN) with $k = 1$. It predicts each student's outcome by finding the single most similar student in the training data and copying their answer. On the training data, every student's nearest neighbour is themselves, so it can never be wrong:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("knn1_pipe ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" Pipeline([");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("\"preprocessor\"");#NormalTok(", preprocessor),");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("\"classifier\"");#NormalTok(", KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("knn1_pipe.fit(X_train, y_train)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("knn1_train_auc ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" roc_auc_score(y_train, knn1_pipe.predict_proba(X_train)[:, ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("knn1_test_auc ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" roc_auc_score(y_test, knn1_pipe.predict_proba(X_test)[:, ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"KNN (k=1) Training AUC: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("knn1_train_auc");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"KNN (k=1) Test AUC:     ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("knn1_test_auc");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("KNN (k=1) Training AUC: 1.00");],
[#NormalTok("KNN (k=1) Test AUC:     0.61");],));
]
]
The model scores perfectly on the training data, and much more poorly on new students. The training score was an illusion: the model had memorised the training students, not learned anything that carries over. It is the degree-12 curve again, and the reason every model in this book is tested on data it has not seen.

== Cross-validation
<cross-validation>
The test set is used only once, at the end. During model building, however, models and settings often need to be compared, and neither the training data (that would reward overfitting) nor the test set (that would use it up) can be used to judge them. #strong[Cross-validation] solves this by reusing the training data. The training data is split into, say, 10 equal parts, called #strong[folds]. The model is fitted on 9 folds and its performance measured on the fold left out; this is repeated 10 times, leaving out each fold in turn, and the 10 performance measures are averaged. Every student is used for evaluation exactly once, always by a model that did not see them during fitting. #ref(<fig-folds>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the idea with 5 folds.

#figure([
#box(image("11-machine-learning_files/figure-typst/fig-folds-output-1.svg", alt: "A grid of five rows, one per round, each split into five blocks. In each row, a different block is dark blue, marking the fold used for evaluation; the other four are grey."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Five-fold cross-validation: in each round, the model is fitted on four folds (grey) and evaluated on the fifth (dark blue).
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-folds>


The class #NormalTok("StratifiedKFold()"); creates stratified folds, and #NormalTok("cross_validate()"); fits and evaluates the pipeline across each fold:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("cv ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" StratifiedKFold(n_splits");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(", shuffle");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("True");#NormalTok(", random_state");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("cv_results ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" cross_validate(logistic_pipe, X_train, y_train, cv");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("cv, scoring");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("[");#StringTok("\"roc_auc\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"accuracy\"");#NormalTok("])");],
[],
[#NormalTok("cv_auc_mean ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" cv_results[");#StringTok("\"test_roc_auc\"");#NormalTok("].mean()");],
[#NormalTok("cv_auc_se ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" cv_results[");#StringTok("\"test_roc_auc\"");#NormalTok("].std() ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" np.sqrt(");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Cross-validated AUC: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("cv_auc_mean");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok(" (SE: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("cv_auc_se");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok(")\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Cross-validated AUC: 0.806 (SE: 0.020)");],));
]
]
The cross-validated AUC, about 0.81, is an honest estimate of how the model will perform on new students, obtained without touching the test set.

== Tuning a model
<tuning-a-model>
Many models have settings that are not learned from the data but must be chosen beforehand, such as $k$ in k-nearest neighbours or the degree of the polynomials in the simulation. They are called #strong[hyperparameters], to distinguish them from the #strong[parameters], such as regression coefficients, that the model learns. The best value is found by #strong[tuning]: trying several values and comparing them with cross-validation.

A #strong[decision tree] (explained fully in Chapter 12) predicts by asking a series of yes-or-no questions, such as whether stress is above 3.5. Its depth, the number of questions in a row, is a hyperparameter that controls its flexibility. A shallow tree is too simple to capture the patterns and underfits; a very deep tree can fit the training data closely, noise included, and overfits:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tree_pipe ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" Pipeline([");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("\"preprocessor\"");#NormalTok(", preprocessor),");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("\"classifier\"");#NormalTok(", DecisionTreeClassifier(random_state");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(", min_samples_leaf");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("])");],
[],
[#NormalTok("param_grid ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" {");#StringTok("\"classifier__max_depth\"");#NormalTok(": ");#BuiltInTok("list");#NormalTok("(");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("11");#NormalTok("))}");],
[#NormalTok("grid_search ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" GridSearchCV(tree_pipe, param_grid");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("param_grid, cv");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("cv, scoring");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"roc_auc\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("grid_search.fit(X_train, y_train)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("tuning_results ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.DataFrame({");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"max_depth\"");#NormalTok(": ");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("11");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("\"mean_auc\"");#NormalTok(": grid_search.cv_results_[");#StringTok("\"mean_test_score\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("})");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(tuning_results.head(");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(").");#BuiltInTok("round");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("   max_depth  mean_auc");],
[#NormalTok("0          1     0.634");],
[#NormalTok("1          2     0.761");],
[#NormalTok("2          3     0.764");],
[#NormalTok("3          4     0.739");],
[#NormalTok("4          5     0.687");],
[#NormalTok("5          6     0.675");],));
]
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("fig, ax ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" plt.subplots(figsize");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.6");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("ax.plot(tuning_results[");#StringTok("\"max_depth\"");#NormalTok("], tuning_results[");#StringTok("\"mean_auc\"");#NormalTok("], marker");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"o\"");#NormalTok(", color");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("navy, linewidth");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("1.5");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_xticks(");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("11");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_xlabel(");#StringTok("\"Tree depth (max_depth)\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_ylabel(");#StringTok("\"Cross-validated ROC AUC\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_title(");#StringTok("\"Hyperparameter Tuning: Decision Tree Depth\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("plt.tight_layout()");],
[#NormalTok("plt.show()");],));
#figure([
#box(image("11-machine-learning_files/figure-typst/fig-tuning-output-1.svg", alt: "Line chart of AUC against tree depth from 1 to 10. The AUC rises steeply from 0.5 at depth 1 to a peak at a moderate depth, then levels off and dips slightly for deeper trees."))
