The pattern is the new-data curve of #ref(<fig-bias-variance>, supplement: [Figure]) seen from the other side: a tree of depth 1 is no better than chance, the AUC rises as the tree grows, and beyond a moderate depth it levels off and dips slightly as the deeper trees start to overfit. Here, #NormalTok("GridSearchCV"); selects #NormalTok("max_depth ="); 3.

== The final test
<the-final-test>
Only now, with the model chosen, is the test set used. The tuned decision tree is evaluated on the test set, alongside plain logistic regression:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("best_tree ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" grid_search.best_estimator_");],
[#NormalTok("tree_test_auc ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" roc_auc_score(y_test, best_tree.predict_proba(X_test)[:, ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("tree_test_acc ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" accuracy_score(y_test, best_tree.predict(X_test))");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Tuned Tree Test AUC:        ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("tree_test_auc");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok(" (Accuracy: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("tree_test_acc");#SpecialCharTok(":.1%}");#SpecialStringTok(")\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Logistic Regression Test AUC: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("auc_test");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok(" (Accuracy: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("acc");#SpecialCharTok(":.1%}");#SpecialStringTok(")\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Tuned Tree Test AUC:        0.725 (Accuracy: 84.7%)");],
[#NormalTok("Logistic Regression Test AUC: 0.843 (Accuracy: 88.0%)");],));
]
]
The tuned tree reaches an AUC of 0.73; plain logistic regression, 0.84. The more flexible model is not the better one. In this data, the risk of dropout rises steadily with stress and falls steadily with support, so a simple smooth model captures the pattern well, while a tree, which splits the data into rectangular boxes, captures it less well. Trying a simple model first, and making a complex one earn its place, is one of the most useful habits in machine learning.

