== Decision trees
<decision-trees>
A #strong[decision tree] classifies by asking a series of yes-or-no questions, like a flowchart. To see how a tree chooses its questions, take twelve imaginary students:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tiny ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.DataFrame({");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("'stress'");#NormalTok(":  [");#FloatTok("1.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.2");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.1");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.4");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.6");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.2");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.5");#NormalTok("],");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("'support'");#NormalTok(": [");#FloatTok("4.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.2");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.1");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.4");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("1.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.9");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("1.5");#NormalTok("],");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("'dropout'");#NormalTok(": [");#StringTok("'No'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'No'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'No'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'No'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'No'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'No'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'Yes'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'No'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'Yes'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'Yes'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'No'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'Yes'");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("})");],));
]
Four of the twelve consider dropping out. The tree looks for the single question that best separates the "Yes" students from the "No" students. "Is support below 2.75?" puts seven students, all "No", on one side, and five students, four "Yes" and one "No", on the other. The one "No" student among the low-support group has the lowest stress, so a second question, "Is stress 3.1 or more?", separates the rest perfectly:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" sklearn.tree ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" DecisionTreeClassifier, export_text");],
[],
[#NormalTok("tiny_tree ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" DecisionTreeClassifier(min_samples_split");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", criterion");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("'gini'");#NormalTok(", random_state");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("tiny_tree.fit(tiny[[");#StringTok("'stress'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'support'");#NormalTok("]], (tiny[");#StringTok("'dropout'");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("'Yes'");#NormalTok(").astype(");#BuiltInTok("int");#NormalTok("))");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(export_text(tiny_tree, feature_names");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("[");#StringTok("'stress'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'support'");#NormalTok("]))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("|--- support <= 2.75");],
[#NormalTok("|   |--- stress <= 3.10");],
[#NormalTok("|   |   |--- class: 0");],
[#NormalTok("|   |--- stress >  3.10");],
[#NormalTok("|   |   |--- class: 1");],
[#NormalTok("|--- support >  2.75");],
[#NormalTok("|   |--- class: 0");],));
]
]
Each line is a #strong[node]: the question that led to it, and the predicted class (#NormalTok("class: 0"); for No, #NormalTok("class: 1"); for Yes). The final lines are the #strong[leaves]. To classify a new student, you follow the questions from the top until you reach a leaf. To choose each question, the tree tries every predictor and every possible cut-off and picks the one that makes the two groups most "pure", usually measured by the #strong[Gini impurity] (0 when a group contains only one class).

Decision trees have attractive properties: they are easy to explain, they need no dummy variables or normalisation (a question such as "is stress 3.1 or more?" works the same on any scale), and they capture interactions automatically (stress matters here only for low-support students). For the wellbeing data, a preprocessor that fills in missing values without scaling is enough:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tree_pipe ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" Pipeline([");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("'prep'");#NormalTok(", tree_recipe),");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("'model'");#NormalTok(", DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(", min_samples_leaf");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(", random_state");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("])");],
[],
[#NormalTok("tree_fit ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" tree_pipe.fit(X_train, y_train)");],));
]
A depth of 3 keeps the tree small enough to read. #ref(<fig-tree>, supplement: [Figure]) draws it using scikit-learn's #NormalTok("plot_tree()");.

#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" sklearn.tree ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" plot_tree");],
[],
[#NormalTok("feature_names ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" [f.split(");#StringTok("'__'");#NormalTok(")[");#OperatorTok("-");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("] ");#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" f ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" tree_pipe.named_steps[");#StringTok("'prep'");#NormalTok("].get_feature_names_out()]");],
[],
