<fig-tree>


The first question is about #NormalTok("support");, the strongest single predictor. The tree is easy to read, but a single tree has a serious weakness: it is unstable. A slightly different sample of students can produce a completely different tree, and, as Chapter 11 showed, a deep tree overfits. The next method turns this weakness into a strength.

== Random forests
<random-forests>
A #strong[random forest] grows hundreds of trees, each on a slightly different version of the data, and lets them vote. Two sources of randomness make the trees different. Each tree is grown on a #strong[bootstrap sample] of the training data (Chapter 7), students drawn at random with replacement. And at each split, the tree may only choose from a random subset of the predictors; the size of this subset, #NormalTok("max_features"); (analogous to #NormalTok("mtry"); in R), is a hyperparameter.

Each individual tree overfits in its own way, but because the trees differ, many of their errors cancel out when the trees vote. The predicted probability of "Yes" is the share of trees that vote "Yes". Averaging many models in this way is called an #strong[ensemble]\; a random forest is an ensemble of trees @breiman2001.

Scikit-learn's #NormalTok("RandomForestClassifier"); fits random forests efficiently:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" sklearn.ensemble ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" RandomForestClassifier");],
[],
[#NormalTok("forest_pipe ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" Pipeline([");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("'prep'");#NormalTok(", tree_recipe),");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("'model'");#NormalTok(", RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("500");#NormalTok(", random_state");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("])");],
[],
[#NormalTok("forest_fit ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" forest_pipe.fit(X_train, y_train)");],));
]
A forest of 500 trees cannot be drawn, so it is a "black box". #strong[Permutation importance] opens it a little: for each predictor in turn, the values are shuffled randomly, and the drop in the model's accuracy is recorded. Shuffling an important predictor hurts the predictions; shuffling an unimportant one does not. #ref(<fig-importance>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the result.

#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" sklearn.inspection ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" permutation_importance");],
[],
[#NormalTok("imp ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" permutation_importance(forest_fit, X_train, y_train, n_repeats");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(", random_state");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("importance_df ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.DataFrame({");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("'predictor'");#NormalTok(": X_train.columns,");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("'importance'");#NormalTok(": imp.importances_mean");],
[#NormalTok("}).sort_values(");#StringTok("'importance'");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("fig, ax ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" plt.subplots(figsize");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.5");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("ax.barh(importance_df[");#StringTok("'predictor'");#NormalTok("], importance_df[");#StringTok("'importance'");#NormalTok("], color");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("'#2f6793'");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.set_xlabel(");#StringTok("'Permutation importance (drop in accuracy)'");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("ax.grid(axis");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("'x'");#NormalTok(", linestyle");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("'--'");#NormalTok(", alpha");#OperatorTok("=");#FloatTok("0.5");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("plt.show()");],));
#figure([
#box(image("12-classification_files/figure-typst/fig-importance-output-1.svg", alt: "Horizontal bar chart of predictor importance. Support and stress have the longest bars, followed by burnout; the demographic variables have bars near zero."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Permutation importance of each predictor in the random forest.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
