<fig-importance>


The forest relies most on support, stress, and burnout, and hardly at all on the students' background, which agrees with the logistic regression of Chapter 8. Importance says which predictors the model #emph[uses], not what #emph[causes] dropout; everything Chapter 8 said about confounding still applies.

== k-nearest neighbours
<k-nearest-neighbours>
#strong[k-nearest neighbours] (k-NN), met briefly in Chapter 11, classifies a new student by finding the $k$ most similar students in the training data and letting them vote. "Similar" means close together when the predictors are drawn as a map. Suppose a new student in the tiny example has a stress score of 3.5 and a support score of 3.0. The #strong[distance] to each training student is the straight-line distance on the stress-support map:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tiny_dist ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" tiny.copy()");],
[#NormalTok("tiny_dist[");#StringTok("'distance'");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.sqrt((tiny_dist[");#StringTok("'stress'");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("3.5");#NormalTok(")");#OperatorTok("**");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" (tiny_dist[");#StringTok("'support'");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(")");#OperatorTok("**");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("tiny_dist.sort_values(");#StringTok("'distance'");#NormalTok(").head(");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(")");],));
#table(
  columns: 5,
  align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,),
  table.header(table.cell(align: right)[], table.cell(align: right)[stress], table.cell(align: right)[support], table.cell(align: right)[dropout], table.cell(align: right)[distance],),
  table.hline(),
  table.cell(align: horizon)[9], [4.0], [2.5], [Yes], [0.707107],
  table.cell(align: horizon)[5], [3.1], [3.8], [No], [0.894427],
  table.cell(align: horizon)[6], [3.4], [2.1], [Yes], [0.905539],
)
With $k = 3$, two of the three nearest students considered dropping out, so the predicted probability of "Yes" is 2/3. #ref(<fig-knn>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the picture.

#figure([
#box(image("12-classification_files/figure-typst/fig-knn-output-1.svg", alt: "Scatter plot of support against stress for twelve students, coloured Yes or No. A cross marks a new student at stress 3.5 and support 3. A circle around the cross encloses three students: two Yes and one No."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
k-nearest neighbours with k = 3. The new student (cross) is classified by the three closest students (circled).
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-knn>


Because k-NN is based on distances, the predictors must be on the same scale. Otherwise a variable measured in large numbers, such as caffeine in milligrams, would dominate the distance, and a variable measured on a 1-5 scale would hardly count. That is why the recipe normalises every predictor.

The number of neighbours, $k$, is a hyperparameter. With $k = 1$, the model copies the nearest student and overfits badly (Chapter 11); with a large $k$, it averages over many students and becomes smoother. Tuning shows which works best for the wellbeing data:

#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" sklearn.model_selection ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" GridSearchCV");],
[#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" sklearn.neighbors ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" KNeighborsClassifier");],
[],
[#NormalTok("knn_pipe ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" Pipeline([");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("'prep'");#NormalTok(", dropout_recipe),");],
[#NormalTok("    (");#StringTok("'knn'");#NormalTok(", KNeighborsClassifier())");],
[#NormalTok("])");],
[],
[#NormalTok("param_grid ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" {");#StringTok("'knn__n_neighbors'");#NormalTok(": [");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("5");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("11");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("21");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("41");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("81");#NormalTok("]}");],
[#NormalTok("knn_grid ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" GridSearchCV(knn_pipe, param_grid");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("param_grid, cv");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("dropout_folds, scoring");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("'roc_auc'");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("knn_grid.fit(X_train, y_train)");],
[],
