With the usual 0.5 threshold, the balanced model has a sensitivity of about #NormalTok("71%");, far higher than the unweighted default (#NormalTok("28%");). But its AUC, #NormalTok("0.81");, has not improved. Weighting the classes did not make the model better at telling students apart; it pushed all the predicted probabilities upwards, which has much the same effect as lowering the threshold. That is often how it works out, and for a model that produces probabilities, choosing the threshold directly is simpler and keeps the probabilities meaningful (after reweighting or upsampling, an output score of 0.6 no longer corresponds to a 60% empirical rate). Class balancing is most useful for models that do not produce calibrated probabilities, and when the rare class is very rare.

