Each #strong[error] (or residual) is the actual value minus the prediction, and three measures summarise them. The #strong[MAE] (mean absolute error) is the average size of the errors, ignoring their sign; here, (0.2 + 0.1 + 0.3 + 0.1 + 0.4) / 5 = 0.22 GPA points. The #strong[RMSE] (root mean squared error) squares the errors, averages them, and takes the square root. Squaring gives large errors more weight, so the RMSE is always at least as large as the MAE, and much larger when there are a few big misses. #strong[R²] is the coefficient of determination: the share of the variation in the outcome that the predictions capture, from 0 to 1. Scikit-learn calculates all three:

#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" sklearn.metrics ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" mean_absolute_error, root_mean_squared_error, r2_score");],
[],
[#NormalTok("pd.DataFrame({");],
