On its own 60 students, the model looks excellent: an RMSE of #NormalTok("0.068");. On new students, its RMSE jumps to #NormalTok("0.664");, #emph[worse than predicting the average GPA for everyone]. With #NormalTok("48"); coefficients estimated from 60 students, the model has enough freedom to fit the noise in its sample, and the coefficients that fit the noise do not carry over. In the extreme, with as many predictors as students, a regression can fit its data perfectly and predict nothing.

The problem is made worse by #strong[multicollinearity]: many of the predictors are strongly correlated (six stress items, two semesters of GPA), and regression struggles to divide the credit between correlated predictors. Their coefficients become large, unstable, and sometimes of opposite signs, cancelling each other out on the training data but not on new data.

== Regularised regression
<regularised-regression>
#strong[Regularisation] tackles this directly: it fits the regression while penalising large coefficients. Ordinary regression chooses the coefficients that minimise the sum of squared errors. Regularised regression minimises:

$ upright("sum of squared errors") + lambda times upright("size of the coefficients") . $

The #strong[penalty] $lambda$ (alpha in scikit-learn) sets how much large coefficients cost. With $lambda = 0$, this is ordinary regression; as $lambda$ grows, the coefficients are pulled, or #strong[shrunk], towards zero. A little shrinkage costs almost nothing in fit to the training data, but makes the model much more stable on new data. Because the penalty depends on the size of the coefficients, the predictors must be on the same scale, which the pipeline's #NormalTok("StandardScaler"); ensures.

There are three versions, which differ in how "size" is measured:

- #strong[Ridge regression] (#NormalTok("Ridge");): uses the sum of the #emph[squared] coefficients ($L_2$ penalty). It shrinks all coefficients towards zero and shares credit among correlated predictors, keeping every predictor in the model.
- #strong[Lasso] (#NormalTok("Lasso");): uses the sum of the #emph[absolute] coefficients ($L_1$ penalty) @tibshirani1996. It shrinks too, but it also sets some coefficients to exactly zero, removing those predictors and performing variable selection.
- #strong[Elastic Net] (#NormalTok("ElasticNet");): combines both penalties with an #NormalTok("l1_ratio"); parameter running from 0 (ridge) to 1 (lasso).

The 60 students are now refitted with a lasso:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" sklearn.linear_model ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" Lasso");],
[],
