== Boosting
<boosting>
The second family builds a model in a completely different way. #strong[Boosting] starts with a very simple prediction, the average, and then adds small decision trees one at a time, each fitted to the #emph[errors] of the model so far. The first tree learns the biggest pattern in the errors; the next tree learns what the first missed; and so on. Each tree's contribution is scaled down by a #strong[learning rate] (for example 0.03), so the model improves in small steps and no single tree dominates. Hundreds of trees, each weak on its own, add up to a strong model.

#ref(<fig-boosting>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the idea with a single predictor, semester 2 GPA, and trees with a single split:

#figure([
#box(image("13-predictive-regression_files/figure-typst/fig-boosting-output-1.svg", alt: "Three panels of final GPA against semester 2 GPA, with the training students as grey points. In the first panel, the prediction is a single step; in the second, a staircase; in the third, a finely stepped curve."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Boosting with one predictor. Predicted final GPA from semester 2 GPA after 1, 10, and 100 trees, each with a single split.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-boosting>


Boosting is closely related to random forests, which also combine many trees. The difference is that a forest grows its trees #emph[independently] and averages them, while boosting grows them #emph[in sequence], each correcting the last.

Scikit-learn provides #NormalTok("GradientBoostingRegressor");. Its main hyperparameters are the number of trees, the learning rate, and the depth of each tree:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gb_grid ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" GridSearchCV(");],
[#NormalTok("    Pipeline([(");#StringTok("'prep'");#NormalTok(", gpa_recipe), (");#StringTok("'model'");#NormalTok(", GradientBoostingRegressor(n_estimators");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("500");#NormalTok(", random_state");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok("))]),");],
[#NormalTok("    param_grid");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("{");#StringTok("'model__learning_rate'");#NormalTok(": [");#FloatTok("0.01");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("0.03");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("0.1");#NormalTok("], ");#StringTok("'model__max_depth'");#NormalTok(": [");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok("]},");],
[#NormalTok("    cv");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("gpa_folds, scoring");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("'neg_root_mean_squared_error'");],
[#NormalTok(").fit(X_train, y_train)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("best_gb_rmse ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok("gb_grid.best_score_");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Best Gradient Boosting RMSE: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("best_gb_rmse");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok(" with params: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("gb_grid");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("best_params_");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Best Gradient Boosting RMSE: 0.209 with params: {'model__learning_rate': 0.01, 'model__max_depth': 1}");],));
]
]
The best settings use trees of depth 1 and a learning rate of 0.01. Trees of depth 1 use one predictor each, adding up separate effects with no interactions. That the shallowest trees work best is another sign that the wellbeing data has no strong interactions for the trees to find.

