A wait of 50 minutes almost certainly belongs to the short group, and 85 minutes to the long group, but 67 minutes is uncertain. These #strong[membership probabilities] (also called soft assignments) are the main advantage of mixture models: they say not just which group a case is in, but how sure that is.

=== Choosing the number of clusters with BIC
<choosing-the-number-of-clusters-with-bic>
With more variables, each group is described by a centre and a #strong[covariance matrix], which sets its shape: round or elongated, and tilted in any direction. In #NormalTok("scikit-learn");, #NormalTok("covariance_type"); can be #NormalTok("'spherical'"); (round), #NormalTok("'diag'"); (axis-aligned ellipses), #NormalTok("'tied'"); (all clusters share the same covariance), or #NormalTok("'full'"); (each cluster has its own flexible covariance matrix).

To choose among them, it uses the #strong[Bayesian information criterion] (BIC). BIC rewards a model for fitting the data well and penalises it for every parameter it needs, so a more complex model must earn its extra parameters. (In scikit-learn, #emph[lower] BIC indicates a better balance of fit and parsimony.) For the profile data:

