Two kinds of unusual student stand out. Among the noise points, 5 students study around 60 hours a week, sleep about 4 hours a night or less, drink over 700 mg of caffeine a day (about seven cups of coffee), and hardly exercise: more extreme than anyone in the overloaded profile. 5 others are the opposite: close to 10 hours of sleep, very few study hours, very little caffeine, and exercise almost every day. The remaining few are extreme versions of the overloaded profile. In Chapter 6, extreme values were checked one variable at a time; DBSCAN finds students who are unusual in their #emph[combination] of values.

These students should not be deleted: they are real students, and the extreme workers are exactly the students a wellbeing service would want to know about. They are reported separately, as students who do not fit the profiles, and the profiles are checked for whether they change when these students are left out.

== Evaluating a clustering
<evaluating-a-clustering>
In supervised learning, a model is judged against the true answers. In clustering there are usually no true answers, so evaluation rests on several kinds of evidence, none decisive on its own.

#strong[Internal measures] judge how compact and well separated the clusters are, using the data alone. The #strong[silhouette] of Chapter 9 is the most common, and BIC plays this role for mixture models. #NormalTok("silhouette_score"); in scikit-learn calculates it:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" sklearn.metrics ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" silhouette_score");],
[],
[#NormalTok("sil_km ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" silhouette_score(profile_data, kmeans_clusters.labels_)");],
[#NormalTok("sil_gmm ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" silhouette_score(profile_data, profiles[");#StringTok("'gmm_cluster'");#NormalTok("])");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"K-means silhouette: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sil_km");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"GMM silhouette: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sil_gmm");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("K-means silhouette: 0.21");],
[#NormalTok("GMM silhouette: 0.23");],));
]
]
Both averages are low (the silhouette runs from −1 to 1), which confirms what DBSCAN suggested: the profiles overlap, and no clustering of this data will be crisp.

#strong[Agreement between methods] asks whether different reasonable methods find similar groups. The #strong[adjusted Rand index] (ARI) measures the agreement between two clusterings. It counts the pairs of cases that both clusterings put together or both put apart, and adjusts for the agreement expected by chance: 1 means identical groupings (whatever the labels), and 0 means no more agreement than chance. A tiny example shows that the labels themselves do not matter:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("first  ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" [");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("second ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" [");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("third  ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" [");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok("]");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"ARI(first, second):\"");#NormalTok(", adjusted_rand_score(first, second))");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"ARI(first, third):\"");#NormalTok(", ");#BuiltInTok("round");#NormalTok("(adjusted_rand_score(first, third), ");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("ARI(first, second): 1.0");],
[#NormalTok("ARI(first, third): 0.242");],));
]
]
The first two clusterings group the six people identically, so their ARI is 1, even though the labels differ. The third splits them differently, so its ARI is much lower. For the two methods applied to the profile data:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("ari_methods ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" adjusted_rand_score(kmeans_clusters.labels_, profiles[");#StringTok("'gmm_cluster'");#NormalTok("])");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"ARI (K-means vs GMM): ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("ari_methods");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("ARI (K-means vs GMM): 0.68");],));
]
]
An ARI of 0.68: substantial agreement, especially given that one solution has four clusters and the other three.

#strong[External validation] compares clusters with known categories, also using the ARI. It is only possible when such categories exist, for example when clustering flowers of known species to test a method. For real profiles there is no answer key, which is exactly why they are being sought.

#strong[Stability] asks whether the clusters survive small changes: a different random start, a bootstrap sample of the students, or leaving out the unusual students. Clusters that appear only under one setting should not be reported.

#strong[Interpretability and usefulness] matter most in the end. The clusters should make sense in the light of theory, and they should differ on outcomes they were not built from, such as GPA or considering dropout. The second point can be checked directly:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("    profiles.merge(students[[");#StringTok("'student_id'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'considering_dropout'");#NormalTok("]], on");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("'student_id'");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    .groupby(");#StringTok("'gmm_cluster'");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    .agg(");],
[#NormalTok("        students");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("'student_id'");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("'count'");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("        considering_dropout");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("'considering_dropout'");#NormalTok(", ");#KeywordTok("lambda");#NormalTok(" x: ");#BuiltInTok("round");#NormalTok("(np.mean(x ");#OperatorTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("'Yes'");#NormalTok("), ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("    )");],
[#NormalTok("    .reset_index()");],
[#NormalTok(")");],));
#table(
  columns: 4,
  align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,),
  table.header(table.cell(align: right)[], table.cell(align: right)[gmm\_cluster], table.cell(align: right)[students], table.cell(align: right)[considering\_dropout],),
  table.hline(),
  table.cell(align: horizon)[0], [1], [212], [0.04],
