== A single neuron
<a-single-neuron>
A #strong[neuron] (or unit) in a neural network does three things. It multiplies each input by a #strong[weight], adds up the results together with a constant called the #strong[bias], and passes the total through an #strong[activation function], which turns it into the neuron's output.

Take a neuron with two inputs, a student's stress and support scores, with weights 1.2 and −0.8 and a bias of −4. For a student with stress 4 and support 2, the total is

$ - 4 + 1.2 times 4 - 0.8 times 2 = - 0.8 . $

The activation function here is the #strong[sigmoid] (also called the logistic function), which squeezes any number into the range 0 to 1:

$ upright("sigmoid") \( z \) = frac(1, 1 + e^(- z)) . $

In Python:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#KeywordTok("def");#NormalTok(" sigmoid(z):");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#ControlFlowTok("return");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" np.exp(");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok("z))");],
[],
[#KeywordTok("def");#NormalTok(" neuron(stress, support):");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#ControlFlowTok("return");#NormalTok(" sigmoid(");#OperatorTok("-");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("1.2");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" stress ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.8");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" support)");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#BuiltInTok("round");#NormalTok("(neuron(stress");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(", support");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("), ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("))");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#BuiltInTok("round");#NormalTok("(neuron(stress");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", support");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok("), ");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("0.31");],
[#NormalTok("0.008");],));
]
]
The high-stress, low-support student gets an output of 0.31; the low-stress, high-support student, 0.008. If the output is read as the probability of considering dropout, this neuron is exactly a #strong[logistic regression] (Chapter 8): the bias is the intercept, the weights are the coefficients, and the sigmoid turns the log odds into a probability. Everything a single neuron can do, Chapter 8 has already done.

== From neurons to networks
<from-neurons-to-networks>
The power of neural networks comes from connecting many neurons in #strong[layers]. In the most common design, the #strong[multilayer perceptron], the inputs feed into a #strong[hidden layer] of neurons, each with its own weights; the outputs of the hidden neurons then feed into an #strong[output layer], which produces the prediction. #ref(<fig-network>, supplement: [Figure]) shows a network with three inputs and three hidden neurons.

#figure([
#block[
#box(image("15-neural-networks_files\\figure-typst\\mermaid-figure-1.png", height: 3.83in, width: 3.04in))

]
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
A neural network with one hidden layer. Every arrow carries a weight; each hidden neuron and the output neuron add up their weighted inputs and apply an activation function.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-network>


Each hidden neuron learns its own combination of the inputs: one might respond to high stress, another to the combination of low support and short sleep. The output neuron then combines these. Because each neuron applies a curved activation function, the network as a whole can represent curved relationships and interactions that a single logistic regression cannot. With enough hidden neurons, a network can approximate almost any relationship between inputs and output. That flexibility is its strength, and, as you will see, its danger.

The activation function matters. #ref(<fig-activation>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the two most common: the sigmoid, used in this chapter, and the #strong[ReLU] (rectified linear unit), which simply replaces negative totals with zero and is the standard choice in deep networks.

#figure([
#box(image("15-neural-networks_files/figure-typst/fig-activation-output-1.svg", alt: "Two panels. Left: the sigmoid, an S-shaped curve rising from 0 on the left to 1 on the right, crossing 0.5 at an input of 0. Right: the ReLU, flat at 0 for negative inputs, then a straight line rising for positive inputs."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Two activation functions: the sigmoid squeezes any input into the range 0 to 1; the ReLU sets negative inputs to 0 and passes positive inputs unchanged.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-activation>


=== How a network learns
<how-a-network-learns>
A network starts with small random weights, so its first predictions are useless. #strong[Training] adjusts the weights step by step to reduce the prediction error on the training data. At each step, an algorithm works out, for every weight, whether a small increase or decrease would reduce the error (the #strong[gradient]), and moves all the weights a little in the helpful direction. This is #strong[gradient descent]. One pass through the training data is an #strong[epoch], and training usually runs for hundreds of epochs.

The idea is clearest when one step is worked by hand. Take a single neuron, which is a logistic regression, and six students, with their stress measured from the group's average:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("six ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.DataFrame({");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("'stress'");#NormalTok(":  np.array([");#FloatTok("2.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.5");#NormalTok("]) ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("3.25");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#StringTok("'dropout'");#NormalTok(": np.array([");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(",   ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(",   ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(",   ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(",   ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(",   ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("})");],));
]
The error to be reduced is the #strong[log loss], the measure of fit that logistic regression uses: it is small when the neuron gives high probabilities to the students who did consider dropping out and low probabilities to those who did not. Training starts with both weights at zero, so every student receives a probability of 0.5:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#KeywordTok("def");#NormalTok(" log_loss(b0, b1):");],
[#NormalTok("    p ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sigmoid(b0 ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" b1 ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" six[");#StringTok("'stress'");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#ControlFlowTok("return");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok("np.mean(six[");#StringTok("'dropout'");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" np.log(p) ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" six[");#StringTok("'dropout'");#NormalTok("]) ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" np.log(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" p))");],
[],
[#NormalTok("b0 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.0");],
[#NormalTok("b1 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.0");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#BuiltInTok("round");#NormalTok("(log_loss(b0, b1), ");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("0.693");],));
]
]
For this neuron, the gradient has a simple form: for each weight, the average of the prediction errors (predicted probability minus the actual outcome), each multiplied by the input that the weight belongs to (1 for the bias):

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("p ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sigmoid(b0 ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" b1 ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" six[");#StringTok("'stress'");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("gradient_b0 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.mean(p ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" six[");#StringTok("'dropout'");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("gradient_b1 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.mean((p ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" six[");#StringTok("'dropout'");#NormalTok("]) ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" six[");#StringTok("'stress'");#NormalTok("])");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"gradient_b0: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("gradient_b0");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok(", gradient_b1: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("gradient_b1");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gradient_b0: 0.000, gradient_b1: -0.292");],));
]
]
The gradient for the stress weight is negative, which means that increasing the weight would reduce the error: the students who considered dropping out have higher stress, and the neuron does not yet know it. One step of gradient descent moves each weight against its gradient, by an amount set by the #strong[learning rate]:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("learning_rate ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("1.0");],
[#NormalTok("b0 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" b0 ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" learning_rate ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" gradient_b0");],
[#NormalTok("b1 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" b1 ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" learning_rate ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" gradient_b1");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"b0: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("b0");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok(", b1: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("b1");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok(", loss: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("log_loss(b0, b1)");#SpecialCharTok(":.3f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("b0: 0.000, b1: 0.292, loss: 0.616");],));
]
]
After one step, the stress weight has become positive and the loss has fallen from 0.693 to 0.671. Training is nothing more than this step, repeated. The loop below repeats it 2,000 times and records the loss:

#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" sklearn.linear_model ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" LogisticRegression");],
[],
[#NormalTok("b0 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.0");],
[#NormalTok("b1 ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.0");],
[#NormalTok("loss_history ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.zeros(");#DecValTok("2000");#NormalTok(")");],
[#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" step ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2000");#NormalTok("):");],
[#NormalTok("    p ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sigmoid(b0 ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" b1 ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" six[");#StringTok("'stress'");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("    b0 ");#OperatorTok("-=");#NormalTok(" learning_rate ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" np.mean(p ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" six[");#StringTok("'dropout'");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("    b1 ");#OperatorTok("-=");#NormalTok(" learning_rate ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" np.mean((p ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" six[");#StringTok("'dropout'");#NormalTok("]) ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" six[");#StringTok("'stress'");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("    loss_history[step] ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" log_loss(b0, b1)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("lr_unpenalized ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" LogisticRegression(penalty");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("None");#NormalTok(", solver");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("'lbfgs'");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("lr_unpenalized.fit(six[[");#StringTok("'stress'");#NormalTok("]], six[");#StringTok("'dropout'");#NormalTok("])");],
[],
[#NormalTok("pd.DataFrame({");],
