<fig-loss>


After 2,000 small steps, the weights found by gradient descent are the coefficients that standard logistic regression calculates. A neural network with thousands of weights is trained in exactly this way. The gradients are harder to write down, and a method called backpropagation calculates them efficiently, but each step still moves every weight a little in the direction that reduces the error.

Two consequences follow. First, because training starts from random weights, two runs can give slightly different networks, so #NormalTok("random_state"); matters. Second, a network with many weights can keep reducing its training error long after it has learned the real pattern, by memorising the noise. The usual defence is #strong[weight decay]: an L2 penalty on large weights, exactly like the ridge penalty of Chapter 13. In scikit-learn, this hyperparameter is called #NormalTok("alpha");.

