In pandas, a time series is stored with a dedicated #strong[index] representing time (either integers representing regular intervals or a #NormalTok("DatetimeIndex");). The observations are spaced regularly, and operations maintain temporal ordering. There is a clear weekly pattern: busy at the start of the week, quiet at the weekend.

=== The parts of a pattern
<the-parts-of-a-pattern>
A time series is easiest to understand as a sum of parts. The code below builds an artificial weekly series for three years from three ingredients: a trend that rises slowly, a seasonal pattern that repeats every 52 weeks, and random noise. #ref(<fig-parts>, supplement: [Figure]) shows each part and their sum.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("np.random.seed(");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("weeks ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.arange(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("157");#NormalTok(")");],
