In the week of 23 October 2023, there were 71 visits, about 30 more than trend and season would predict. When asked, the doctors remember at once: it was the university's mental health awareness week, with posters everywhere encouraging students to seek help. The remainder is where such events show up. It is a real week, not an error, so it is kept, and the robust decomposition makes sure it does not distort the forecasts.

Notice also that the seasonal swings grow slightly as the trend rises: the peaks get higher and the summer lows stay low. When the seasonal pattern grows in proportion to the level of the series, it is standard practice to model the #strong[logarithm] of the series, on which the swings become additive and stable.

== Forecasting
<forecasting>
=== Simple benchmarks
<simple-benchmarks>
Every forecasting method should be compared with simple #strong[benchmark] methods, just as every predictive model in Chapters 11 to 13 was compared with a baseline. The three most common are:

+ #strong[Mean method]: forecasts the average of all past observations.
+ #strong[Naive method]: forecasts the last observed value ($y_T$).
+ #strong[Seasonal naive method]: forecasts the value from the same season in the last cycle ($y_(T + h - m)$).

For the clinic example, with its weekly cycle of 7 days:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("y_clinic ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" clinic[");#StringTok("'visits'");#NormalTok("].values");],
[],
[#NormalTok("mean_fc   ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.repeat(np.mean(y_clinic), ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(")");],
