=== Two families of models
<two-families-of-models>
Two families of models go beyond the benchmarks:

- #strong[Exponential smoothing (ETS)]: forecasts with weighted averages of past observations, with weights that decrease exponentially into the past so that recent weeks count most. ETS models can track a changing level, trend, and seasonality.
- #strong[ARIMA]: models describe how each observation depends on earlier observations (autoregression) and on earlier random shocks (moving average), using the autocorrelation structure of the series.

Both work best with short seasonal periods, such as 4 quarters, 7 days, or 12 months. A 52-week season is long, so two strategies are common: 1. #strong[Decompose first (STL + ETS)]: decompose the log series with STL, forecast the seasonally adjusted series with ETS, and add the seasonal component back. 2. #strong[Fourier terms with ARIMA]: model the 52-week seasonal wave using pairs of sine and cosine terms (Fourier series) as external regressors.

=== Training and test periods
<training-and-test-periods>
As in machine learning, forecasts must be judged on data the model has not seen. For time series, the test data must come #emph[after] the training data, because a forecast can only use the past. The models are trained on the first four academic years (208 weeks) and tested on the fifth (52 weeks):

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("train ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" visits[visits.index ");#OperatorTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("'2024-09-01'");#NormalTok("].copy()");],
