== Reproducibility and credibility
<reproducibility-and-credibility>
An analysis is #strong[reproducible] if someone else, or you in a year's time, can take the same data and code and get exactly the same results. It is #strong[replicable] if a new study, with new data, reaches the same conclusions. Reproducibility is the minimum standard: if the original results cannot even be recomputed, there is little point asking whether they replicate @peng2011.

Most irreproducibility is not fraud but everyday friction: a table copied before the data was corrected, a spreadsheet cell edited by hand, a result produced by clicking through menus that nobody wrote down, a package that changed its default between versions. The previous chapters have already built good habits against these. Every step is #strong[written as code], in Python scripts or notebooks, so it can be rerun (Chapter 1). Each analysis lives in a dedicated project directory with relative paths, so it runs on any computer (Chapter 1). The #strong[raw data is never edited by hand], and every cleaning decision is made and recorded in code (Chapter 3). Random steps use #strong[#NormalTok("np.random.seed()");] or explicit random state generators, so they give the same answer every time (Chapter 7).

This chapter adds the last links: putting the results into the report automatically, fixing the software versions, and sharing everything responsibly. Good practices do not need to be perfect to be useful; even a few of them make research far easier to check @wilson2017.

== Documents that contain their own analysis
<documents-that-contain-their-own-analysis>
#strong[Quarto] is a modern publishing system for documents that mix text and executable code. A Quarto document is a plain text file with the extension #NormalTok(".qmd");. When it is #strong[rendered], the code is executed, and its results (numbers, tables, figures) are placed directly into the finished document, which can be a web page, a Word document, a PDF, a slide show, or an entire book. This book itself is written in Quarto: every number, table, and figure in it was produced by the Python code shown next to it.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
In Python data science, #strong[Jupyter Notebooks] (#NormalTok(".ipynb"); files) are very popular for interactive exploration. However, notebooks store raw output and metadata as bulky JSON, which makes version control in Git difficult. #strong[Quarto] (#NormalTok(".qmd"); files) combines the best of both worlds: plain-text Markdown that is clean to track in Git, paired with a Jupyter kernel underneath that runs your Python code when rendering. Quarto works natively with Python, R, Julia, and Observable JavaScript. For reproducible thesis chapters and reports, Quarto plain text is the gold standard.

]
