=== A small example
<a-small-example-2>
A complete Quarto document can be very short:

#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("---");],
[#AnnotationTok("title:");#CommentTok(" \"Three friends' sleep\"");],
[#AnnotationTok("author:");#CommentTok(" \"Elaf\"");],
[#AnnotationTok("format:");#CommentTok(" html");],
[#AnnotationTok("jupyter:");#CommentTok(" python3");],
[#CommentTok("---");],
[],
[#NormalTok("My three friends slept 6.3 hours on average last night.");],
[],
[#InformationTok("```{python}");],
[#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" numpy ");#ImportTok("as");#NormalTok(" np");],
[#NormalTok("sleep ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" [");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("7.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("5.5");#NormalTok("]");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#BuiltInTok("round");#NormalTok("(np.mean(sleep), ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("))");],
[#InformationTok("```");],));
It has the three parts of every Quarto document:

+ The #strong[YAML header], between the two #NormalTok("---"); lines, holds settings: the title, the author, the output #strong[format], and the Python kernel (#NormalTok("jupyter: python3");).
+ The #strong[text] is written in #strong[Markdown], a simple way of styling text: #NormalTok("**bold**");, #NormalTok("*italic*");, #NormalTok("# Heading");, and #NormalTok("-"); for bullet points.
+ The #strong[code chunks], between #NormalTok("{python}"); and #NormalTok("```");, are executed when the document is rendered, and their outputs appear in the document.

The text can also contain #strong[inline code], such as #NormalTok("<code>{python} mean_sleep</code>");: Python variables evaluated directly inside a sentence. When the document is rendered, the inline code is evaluated and replaced by its numerical or text result. If a friend's number changes in the data, the sentence changes with it. Inline code is the cure for the problem at the start of the chapter: numbers in the text are never typed by hand.

In VS Code or Positron, you can edit Quarto documents with syntax highlighting, run code chunks interactively, and render them with a single click or keyboard shortcut.

=== What happens when you render
<what-happens-when-you-render>
#ref(<fig-render>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the steps. The #strong[Jupyter] execution engine runs the Python code and produces Markdown with the output figures and tables in place; #strong[Pandoc], a universal document converter, then transforms the Markdown into the requested format.

#figure([
#block[
#box(image("17-reproducible-research_files\\figure-typst\\mermaid-figure-1.png", height: 6.06in, width: 4.74in))

]
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
How a Quarto document is rendered with Python.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-render>


Because the code is run from the beginning every time, a rendered document is a test of reproducibility in itself: if the code depends on an unrecorded variable created in an interactive console, rendering fails immediately, alerting you to the missing step.

== A results chapter in Quarto
<a-results-chapter-in-quarto>
The descriptive results chapter of the thesis, written as a Quarto document, begins like this:

#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("---");],
[#AnnotationTok("title:");#CommentTok(" \"Chapter 4: Results\"");],
[#AnnotationTok("author:");#CommentTok(" \"Elaf\"");],
