In Python, Quarto passes the parameters into a dictionary named #NormalTok("params");. Rendering from the command line with #NormalTok("-P"); outputs customized reports for each faculty:

#Skylighting(([#ExtensionTok("quarto");#NormalTok(" render faculty-report.qmd ");#AttributeTok("-P");#NormalTok(" faculty:");#StringTok("\"Health Sciences\"");],));
== Keeping the same package versions
<keeping-the-same-package-versions>
Python libraries evolve rapidly. A function default may change, or a method may be deprecated, causing code that runs today to break or produce different estimates two years later. Reproducibility requires pinning exact software dependencies.

=== Virtual environments and requirements.txt
<virtual-environments-and-requirements.txt>
A #strong[virtual environment] is an isolated Python directory containing a specific Python interpreter and its installed packages. By creating an environment per project, updates to packages in one project never break another:

#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# 1. Create a dedicated virtual environment");],
[#ExtensionTok("python");#NormalTok(" ");#AttributeTok("-m");#NormalTok(" venv .venv");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# 2. Activate it (Windows PowerShell)");],
[#ExtensionTok(".venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# 2b. Activate it (macOS/Linux)");],
[#BuiltInTok("source");#NormalTok(" .venv/bin/activate");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# 3. Install required packages");],
[#ExtensionTok("pip");#NormalTok(" install pandas numpy scipy statsmodels scikit-learn matplotlib seaborn");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# 4. Save exact versions to a lockfile");],
[#ExtensionTok("pip");#NormalTok(" freeze ");#OperatorTok(">");#NormalTok(" requirements.txt");],));
Sharing #NormalTok("requirements.txt"); alongside your thesis repository allows another researcher to recreate your exact environment with one command:

#Skylighting(([#ExtensionTok("pip");#NormalTok(" install ");#AttributeTok("-r");#NormalTok(" requirements.txt");],));
Modern Python tooling also offers lockfile tools such as #strong[#NormalTok("uv");] (#NormalTok("uv pip compile"); / #NormalTok("uv.lock");) or #strong[Poetry], which capture complete dependency graphs and cryptographic hashes for maximum reproducibility.

=== Reporting software versions
<reporting-software-versions>
Even without a formal lockfile, always #strong[report the versions] of Python and key analysis libraries in your thesis methods section. In Python, you can inspect versions directly:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" sys");],
[#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" statsmodels");],
[#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" sklearn");],
[#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" pandas ");#ImportTok("as");#NormalTok(" pd");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Python: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sys");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("version");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("split()[");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok("]");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"pandas: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("pd");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("__version__");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"statsmodels: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("statsmodels");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("__version__");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"scikit-learn: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("sklearn");#SpecialCharTok(".");#NormalTok("__version__");#SpecialCharTok("}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Python: 3.13.2");],
[#NormalTok("pandas: 2.3.3");],
[#NormalTok("statsmodels: 0.15.0");],
[#NormalTok("scikit-learn: 1.9.1");],));
]
]
== Interactive dashboards with Shiny for Python
<interactive-dashboards-with-shiny-for-python>
A static thesis report answers only the questions its author anticipated. Readers, reviewers, and policymakers often want to ask their own questions: #emph["What does the trend look like for my faculty?"] or #emph["What if we examine sleep duration rather than overall wellbeing?"]

#strong[Shiny for Python] turns Python data analysis into an interactive web dashboard without requiring HTML, CSS, or JavaScript expertise.

A Shiny app is organized around three concepts:

+ #strong[Inputs]: The interactive UI widgets the user controls (drop-downs, radio buttons, sliders).
+ #strong[Outputs]: The visual elements the app displays (plots, dynamic tables, text cards).
+ #strong[Reactivity]: The engine that links them. When an input changes, Shiny automatically re-executes only the outputs and calculations that depend on that specific input (#ref(<fig-reactivity>, supplement: [Figure])).

#figure([
#block[
#box(image("17-reproducible-research_files\\figure-typst\\mermaid-figure-2.png", height: 2.52in, width: 6.46in))

]
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Reactivity in the wellbeing dashboard. When an input changes, only downstream elements update.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-reactivity>


