=== Sharing dashboards
<sharing-dashboards>
To share an app with stakeholders who do not have Python installed:

- #strong[shinyapps.io]: Free and paid cloud hosting for Shiny apps.
- #strong[Posit Connect]: Enterprise server commonly hosted by universities and research institutes.
- #strong[Shinylive]: An extraordinary technology that runs Shiny for Python entirely inside the user's web browser using WebAssembly (Pyodide), requiring no backend server at all.

== Open science
<open-science>
Reproducibility within your own computer is the foundation. #strong[Open science] broadens the horizon: ensuring research is accessible, inspectable, and reusable by the global scientific community.

=== Sharing data and code
<sharing-data-and-code>
Sharing code and data enables independent verification and accelerates scientific discovery. Trusted repositories such as the #strong[Open Science Framework (OSF)] and #strong[Zenodo] archive research artifacts permanently and assign them a #strong[DOI] (Digital Object Identifier) that can be formally cited in publications.

A complete reproduction package contains:

+ The raw data (or explicit access instructions if restricted).
+ The data cleaning and analysis Python scripts.
+ The Quarto source files (#NormalTok(".qmd");) and bibliography.
+ The environment specification (#NormalTok("requirements.txt"); or #NormalTok("uv.lock");).
+ A comprehensive #strong[codebook] detailing every variable and unit of measurement.
+ An open #strong[license]: CC-BY for data/text and the MIT or Apache 2.0 license for code.

=== Protecting participants and anonymisation
<protecting-participants-and-anonymisation>
Human subject data must never be shared in a form that permits identification. Simply stripping student names and ID numbers is insufficient, because combinations of ordinary demographic variables can uniquely identify individuals:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("small_cells ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" (");],
[#NormalTok("    students.groupby([");#StringTok("\"faculty\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"study_mode\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"gender\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"has_children\"");#NormalTok("], observed");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("False");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    .size()");],
[#NormalTok("    .reset_index(name");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"n\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    .sort_values(");#StringTok("\"n\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("small_cells.head(");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(")");],));
#table(
  columns: 6,
  align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,),
  table.header(table.cell(align: right)[], table.cell(align: right)[faculty], table.cell(align: right)[study\_mode], table.cell(align: right)[gender], table.cell(align: right)[has\_children], table.cell(align: right)[n],),
  table.hline(),
  table.cell(align: horizon)[7], [Education], [Part-time], [Male], [Yes], [3],
  table.cell(align: horizon)[15], [Health Sciences], [Part-time], [Male], [Yes], [3],
  table.cell(align: horizon)[21], [Humanities], [Part-time], [Female], [Yes], [3],
  table.cell(align: horizon)[5], [Education], [Part-time], [Female], [Yes], [4],
)
In the output above, only 3 students are part-time male students with children in the Faculty of Education. In a small academic department, that combination easily identifies real individuals.

To protect participants before public data sharing:

- Group rare categories (e.g., binning ages or combining sparse faculties).
- Top-code or bottom-code extreme outliers.
- Omit unnecessary demographic identifiers.
- When raw data cannot be published due to privacy or ethics restrictions, generate and share a #strong[synthetic dataset] with identical statistical distributions, allowing peers to verify code execution without exposing participant records.

=== Preregistration and the garden of forking paths
<preregistration-and-the-garden-of-forking-paths>
Every empirical study involves dozens of defensible analytical decisions: whether to exclude outliers beyond 2 or 3 standard deviations, whether to log-transform a skewed variable, which non-parametric test to substitute, or which covariates to include in regression models.

When these decisions are made #emph[after] inspecting the data, researchers naturally (and often subconsciously) gravitate toward choices that yield statistically significant results ($p < 0.05$). This phenomenon is known as the #strong[garden of forking paths] @gelman2014.

A Python simulation illustrates the magnitude of this problem. In the simulation below, we generate 60 observations from an exponential distribution with #strong[zero real group difference]. We then analyze the data five defensible ways and record how often the first analysis, and how often #emph[any] of the five analyses, produces a false positive ($p < 0.05$):

#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" numpy ");#ImportTok("as");#NormalTok(" np");],
[#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" pandas ");#ImportTok("as");#NormalTok(" pd");],
[#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" scipy.stats ");#ImportTok("as");#NormalTok(" stats");],
[#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" statsmodels.api ");#ImportTok("as");#NormalTok(" sm");],
[],
[#KeywordTok("def");#NormalTok(" forking_paths():");],
[#NormalTok("    group ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.array([");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("30");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" [");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("30");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    score ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.random.exponential(scale");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(", size");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("60");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    covariate ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.random.normal(size");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("60");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("    ");],
[#NormalTok("    score_a ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" score[group ");#OperatorTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("    score_b ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" score[group ");#OperatorTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("    ");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#CommentTok("# 1. Standard t-test");],
[#NormalTok("    p_ttest ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.ttest_ind(score_a, score_b).pvalue");],
[#NormalTok("    ");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#CommentTok("# 2. Outlier-trimmed t-test (|z| < 2)");],
[#NormalTok("    z ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.");#BuiltInTok("abs");#NormalTok("((score ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" np.mean(score)) ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" np.std(score, ddof");#OperatorTok("=");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("    p_no_outliers ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.ttest_ind(");],
[#NormalTok("        score[(z ");#OperatorTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(") ");#OperatorTok("&");#NormalTok(" (group ");#OperatorTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok(")], ");],
[#NormalTok("        score[(z ");#OperatorTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(") ");#OperatorTok("&");#NormalTok(" (group ");#OperatorTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok(")]");],
[#NormalTok("    ).pvalue");],
[#NormalTok("    ");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#CommentTok("# 3. Log-scale t-test");],
[#NormalTok("    p_log ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.ttest_ind(np.log(score_a), np.log(score_b)).pvalue");],
[#NormalTok("    ");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#CommentTok("# 4. Rank test (Mann-Whitney U)");],
[#NormalTok("    p_rank ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" stats.mannwhitneyu(score_a, score_b).pvalue");],
[#NormalTok("    ");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#CommentTok("# 5. Linear model with covariate");],
[#NormalTok("    X ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sm.add_constant(np.column_stack([(group ");#OperatorTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok(").astype(");#BuiltInTok("int");#NormalTok("), covariate]))");],
[#NormalTok("    p_cov ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" sm.OLS(score, X).fit().pvalues[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("    ");],
[#NormalTok("    p_vals ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" [p_ttest, p_no_outliers, p_log, p_rank, p_cov]");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#ControlFlowTok("return");#NormalTok(" {");],
[#NormalTok("        ");#StringTok("\"first_analysis\"");#NormalTok(": p_ttest ");#OperatorTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.05");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("        ");#StringTok("\"any_analysis\"");#NormalTok(": ");#BuiltInTok("any");#NormalTok("(p ");#OperatorTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.05");#NormalTok(" ");#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" p ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" p_vals)");],
[#NormalTok("    }");],
[],
[#NormalTok("np.random.seed(");#DecValTok("17");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("sim_results ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.DataFrame([forking_paths() ");#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" _ ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("range");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1000");#NormalTok(")])");],
[#NormalTok("sim_results.mean()");],));
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("first_analysis    0.054");],
[#NormalTok("any_analysis      0.109");],
[#NormalTok("dtype: float64");],));
The single pre-planned t-test produces a false positive in approximately 5% of runs, exactly as the nominal $alpha = 0.05$ level dictates. However, choosing opportunistically among five defensible analyses inflates the false positive rate to over 10%---even though the true effect is exactly zero!

#strong[Preregistration] solves this dilemma. Researchers register their hypotheses, experimental design, and planned statistical model on an independent, time-stamped registry (such as OSF Registries or AsPredicted) before collecting or inspecting the data. Preregistration clearly separates #strong[confirmatory] analyses from exploratory findings. In a #strong[Registered Report], a journal peer-reviews and accepts the study protocol before data collection commences, ensuring publication regardless of whether the eventual findings are statistically significant.

=== Citing software
<citing-software>
Open-source scientific Python packages are developed by researchers and community volunteers whose academic careers depend on citations. Always cite the specific packages used in your thesis.

Official citation guidelines and BibTeX records are available on package documentation pages (e.g., McKinney, 2010 for pandas; Pedregosa et al., 2011 for scikit-learn; Virtanen et al., 2020 for SciPy; Seabold & Perktold, 2010 for statsmodels).

A methodology section should state:

#quote(block: true)[
#emph["Analyses were conducted in Python (v3.13), utilizing pandas (v2.2) for data manipulation, SciPy (v1.14) and statsmodels (v0.14) for statistical modeling, and scikit-learn (v1.6) for machine learning."]
]

