== Keeping a history with git (optional)
<keeping-a-history-with-git-optional>
As a thesis project evolves, files quickly proliferate: #NormalTok("thesis_final.qmd");, #NormalTok("thesis_final_v2.qmd");, #NormalTok("thesis_final_supervisor_edits.qmd");. #strong[Version control] replaces file duplication with a clean, linear history of every modification made to the project.

#strong[Git] is the industry-standard version control system, and #strong[GitHub] is the premier platform for cloud backup, team collaboration, and scientific dissemination @bryan2018.

Key Git concepts:

- #strong[Repository]: The project directory whose complete history Git tracks.
- #strong[Commit]: A snapshot of tracked files at a specific moment in time, accompanied by a descriptive message (e.g., #NormalTok("\"Filter duplicate survey responses in ch3\"");).
- #strong[Push / Pull]: Synchronizing local commits with a remote repository on GitHub.
- #strong[#NormalTok(".gitignore");]: A text file specifying files Git must ignore (e.g., raw sensitive data, temporary cache directories, #NormalTok(".venv/");).

Modern code editors (VS Code, Positron, PyCharm) provide intuitive visual Git interfaces with buttons for staging changes, writing commit messages, and pushing to GitHub.

== Common misconceptions
<common-misconceptions-11>
- #strong["Reproducibility is just about sharing a code snippet."] Reproducibility encompasses the full pipeline from raw data to final published estimates, including documentation of every data exclusion, environment dependencies, and random seeds.
- #strong["Copying and pasting a few numbers manually is harmless."] Manual transcriptions are the single most frequent source of discrepancies between analytical code and thesis prose.
- #strong["If every analytical choice is reasonable, the resulting p-value is valid."] Evaluating multiple defensible choices after seeing the data severely inflates false positive rates (the garden of forking paths).
- #strong["Deleting names makes data anonymous."] High-dimensional demographic combinations readily re-identify participants; demographic cell sizes must be scrutinized prior to public release.

== Chapter review
<chapter-review-16>
=== Summary
<summary-16>
- Reproducibility is a pillar of scientific integrity. An analysis is reproducible when independent researchers can run the same code on the same data and obtain identical results.
- Quarto documents (#NormalTok(".qmd");) integrate narrative Markdown, Python code chunks, dynamic tables, plots, LaTeX equations, and BibTeX citations into a unified, reproducible source that compiles to HTML, Word, PDF, or presentations.
- Virtual environments (#NormalTok("venv");, #NormalTok("uv");) and #NormalTok("requirements.txt"); capture library dependencies so that environments can be recreated reliably on any machine.
- Shiny for Python creates reactive dashboards that empower readers to interactively explore findings without modifying code.
- Open science practices include depositing code and data in DOI-assigning archives (OSF, Zenodo), protecting demographic privacy, preregistering confirmatory analyses, and citing open-source tools.
- Git provides robust version control, recording every revision and preventing accidental data or code loss.

