= Using AI with Python
<sec-ch18>
Much research data is text: answers to open questions, interview transcripts, clinical notes, and policy documents. To analyse qualitative text quantitatively, researchers use #strong[qualitative coding]: reading each text passage and assigning it to one of a set of themes defined in a systematic #strong[codebook]. Coding is an act of judgement, and judgements differ. A central methodological question is therefore how to determine whether a coder---whether a human researcher, a keyword dictionary, or a computer model---codes reliably. The standard answer is to compare the coder with an independent benchmark, and to measure how far they agree beyond what chance alone would produce.

In the wellbeing study, the final survey included one open question: #emph["What has been your biggest challenge during your studies?"] A total of 536 students answered in their own words, and the eleventh research question (RQ11) investigates what those challenges are. Elaf coded 200 answers by hand, which required two full weeks of intense concentration. The methodological challenge is whether an AI tool could code the remaining answers reliably.

Artificial intelligence (AI) tools now play a major role across research: coding assistants that write and explain Python code, and large language models (LLMs) that read, summarize, and classify text. This chapter explores both applications. It explains how these models work and why they make confident errors (hallucinations), shows how to use an AI assistant to write robust Python code and how to verify its outputs, and then demonstrates how to call a language model from Python to code students' responses, validating the results against human coding with accuracy and Cohen's kappa. It concludes with essential ethical guidelines for using AI responsibly in scientific research.

