== The reliability of coding
<the-reliability-of-coding>
Two coders who read the same answers will not always choose the same theme. Their raw agreement---the proportion of answers on which they assign identical themes---seems like the natural metric, but it can be highly deceptive. When one theme is very frequent, two coders will often agree simply because both choose it most of the time by chance.

A toy example illustrates why this matters. Suppose two coders categorize 20 student answers, and one coder places 16 of them under Workload:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("coder_a ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" [");#StringTok("\"Workload\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("16");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" [");#StringTok("\"Family\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("4");],
[#NormalTok("coder_b ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" [");#StringTok("\"Workload\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("14");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" [");#StringTok("\"Family\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" [");#StringTok("\"Workload\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" [");#StringTok("\"Family\"");#NormalTok("] ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");],
[],
[#NormalTok("observed ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" np.mean(np.array(coder_a) ");#OperatorTok("==");#NormalTok(" np.array(coder_b))");],
[#NormalTok("p_a ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.Series(coder_a).value_counts(normalize");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("True");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("p_b ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" pd.Series(coder_b).value_counts(normalize");#OperatorTok("=");#VariableTok("True");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("chance ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("sum");#NormalTok("(p_a.get(k, ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(") ");#OperatorTok("*");#NormalTok(" p_b.get(k, ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(") ");#ControlFlowTok("for");#NormalTok(" k ");#KeywordTok("in");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("set");#NormalTok("(coder_a) ");#OperatorTok("|");#NormalTok(" ");#BuiltInTok("set");#NormalTok("(coder_b))");],
[#NormalTok("kappa ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" (observed ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" chance) ");#OperatorTok("/");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok(" chance)");],
[],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Observed agreement: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("observed");#SpecialCharTok(":.1%}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Chance agreement:   ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("chance");#SpecialCharTok(":.1%}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"Cohen's kappa:      ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("kappa");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Observed agreement: 80.0%");],
[#NormalTok("Chance agreement:   68.0%");],
[#NormalTok("Cohen's kappa:      0.37");],));
]
]
The coders agree on 80% of the answers, which initially sounds strong. But because each coder classifies 80% of the responses under Workload, two coders guessing at random in those proportions would agree on 68% of the answers by chance alone!

#strong[Cohen's kappa] measures agreement beyond chance: the observed agreement minus chance agreement, divided by the maximum possible agreement above chance @cohen1960:

$ kappa = frac(P_o - P_e, 1 - P_e) $

Here, Cohen's kappa is only 0.38. A kappa of 0 represents chance-level agreement, and 1 indicates perfect agreement. Standard benchmarks @landis1977 classify $kappa$ as:

- #strong[0.00 -- 0.20]: Slight agreement
- #strong[0.21 -- 0.40]: Fair agreement
- #strong[0.41 -- 0.60]: Moderate agreement
- #strong[0.61 -- 0.80]: Substantial agreement
- #strong[0.81 -- 1.00]: Almost perfect agreement

In Python, the #NormalTok("cohen_kappa_score"); function from #NormalTok("scikit-learn"); computes this directly:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" sklearn.metrics ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" cohen_kappa_score");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialStringTok("f\"scikit-learn kappa: ");#SpecialCharTok("{");#NormalTok("cohen_kappa_score(coder_a, coder_b)");#SpecialCharTok(":.2f}");#SpecialStringTok("\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("scikit-learn kappa: 0.38");],));
]
]
Kappa is the standard measure of #strong[inter-rater reliability] (Chapter 5), and it applies to any pair of coders---whether two human raters, a keyword dictionary, or a language model.

== How AI assistants work
<how-ai-assistants-work>
Modern AI coding assistants (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, GitHub Copilot) are powered by #strong[large language models] (LLMs). An LLM is a deep neural network (Chapter 15) containing billions of parameters, trained on massive corpora of text and source code.

During training, the model learns to perform one core task: predict the next #strong[token] (a word or sub-word piece) given the preceding context. Generating a response proceeds token by token, continually selecting statistically probable continuations. Instruction tuning and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) then shape the raw model into an assistant that follows conversational instructions.

This fundamental architecture explains both their strengths and their hazards:

- #strong[Strengths]: LLMs are remarkably fluent in Python, NumPy, pandas, and scikit-learn because extensive documentation, tutorials, and open-source code were present in their training data. They excel at writing initial boilerplate, translating concepts between languages, explaining obscure error traces, and suggesting alternative library functions.
- #strong[Hazards]: LLMs generate text that is #emph[statistically plausible], not factually verified. A non-existent method name, an obsolete argument, or a subtly incorrect statistical denominator can be generated with total stylistic confidence. Such fabrications are known as #strong[hallucinations]. Furthermore, a model's knowledge ceases at its training cut-off date, and it possesses no intrinsic awareness of your local data structures unless you provide them.

The practical rule is absolute: #strong[treat AI output as a draft, and verify every line yourself.] You remain solely responsible for the integrity of your thesis.

== AI as a coding assistant
<ai-as-a-coding-assistant>
=== Asking effective questions
<asking-effective-questions>
An AI assistant's response is only as precise as the prompt it receives. To obtain reliable Python code:

+ #strong[State the goal clearly]: e.g., #emph["For each faculty, compute the percentage of students who considered dropping out."]
+ #strong[Describe the data structure]: provide column names and types (e.g., using #NormalTok("students.dtypes"); or #NormalTok("students.head(2)");), ensuring no sensitive or identifiable participant data is ever pasted into online prompts.
+ #strong[Specify the preferred libraries]: e.g., #emph["Use pandas groupby and vectorised methods, avoiding deprecated syntax."]
+ #strong[Provide exact error messages]: when debugging, paste the full traceback along with the offending code snippet.

=== Verifying the suggestion
<verifying-the-suggestion>
Suppose an assistant is asked for the percentage of students in each faculty who considered dropping out, and suggests the following pandas code:

#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# Plausible but incorrect suggestion:");],
[#NormalTok("flawed ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" (");],
