The keyword dictionary achieves 61.0% accuracy and a Cohen's kappa of 0.51 (moderate agreement). Keyword matching struggles because semantic meaning depends on syntactic context: the phrase #emph["I feel I am letting down both my family and my supervisor"] matches keywords in both Family and Supervision, while #emph["I never have enough time"] could reflect course workload or family caring duties. Discerning underlying meaning requires semantic comprehension---the exact strength of modern language models.

== Calling a language model from Python
<calling-a-language-model-from-python>
Python provides standard libraries for interacting with language models:

- #strong[Online API services]: Official SDKs (#NormalTok("openai");, #NormalTok("anthropic");, #NormalTok("google-genai");) offer top-tier reasoning capabilities. Online APIs require an API key and incur per-token charges. Keys should always be stored in environment variables (e.g., via a #NormalTok(".env"); file), never committed directly into Git repositories.
- #strong[Local models via Ollama]: The free, open-source #strong[Ollama] platform runs open models (such as Google Gemma, Meta Llama, or Mistral) locally on your machine. The Python #NormalTok("ollama"); package communicates with the local runner. Local models require zero API keys, run completely free of charge, and guarantee that participant data never leaves your computer.

=== Structured outputs with Pydantic
<structured-outputs-with-pydantic>
Qualitative coding requires two properties that standard web chats lack:

+ #strong[System Prompt]: A fixed, reproducible codebook defining the seven themes and explicit tie-breaking rules.
+ #strong[Structured Enforcement]: Guaranteeing that the model's response is strictly restricted to one of the seven valid theme names, avoiding verbose conversational preambles.

In Python, structured outputs are defined using #strong[Pydantic]:

#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" pydantic ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" BaseModel");],
[#ImportTok("from");#NormalTok(" typing ");#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" Literal");],
[],
[#KeywordTok("class");#NormalTok(" QualitativeCoding(BaseModel):");],
[#NormalTok("    theme: Literal[");],
[#NormalTok("        ");#StringTok("\"Supervision\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Workload\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Finances\"");#NormalTok(", ");],
[#NormalTok("        ");#StringTok("\"Family\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Health\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Isolation\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Other\"");],
[#NormalTok("    ]");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# The qualitative codebook prompt:");],
[#NormalTok("codebook ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"\"\"");],
[#StringTok("You are helping an academic researcher code open-ended survey answers from graduate");],
[#StringTok("students, who were asked: 'What has been your biggest challenge during your studies?'");],
[#StringTok("Assign each answer to exactly ONE theme: the single primary challenge the student describes.");],
[#StringTok("If multiple challenges are mentioned, select the one the student presents first or emphasizes most.");],
[],
[#StringTok("Themes:");],
[#StringTok("- Supervision: supervisor guidance, feedback, meetings, availability, or disagreements.");],
[#StringTok("- Workload: too much work or too little time; deadlines, thesis writing, coursework, readings.");],
[#StringTok("- Finances: tuition fees, living expenses, funding, scholarships, debt, paid employment.");],
[#StringTok("- Family: children, spouse, caring responsibilities, family commitments, home life.");],
[#StringTok("- Health: physical illness, mental health, stress, anxiety, exhaustion, burnout, insomnia.");],
[#StringTok("- Isolation: loneliness, social alienation, lack of peer network, studying remotely.");],
[#StringTok("- Other: administrative issues, research ethics approvals, statistical software, language barriers.");],
[#StringTok("\"\"\"");],));
Using Ollama in Python, a classification query is executed with zero sampling temperature (#NormalTok("temperature=0");) to ensure deterministic repeatability:

#Skylighting(([#ImportTok("import");#NormalTok(" ollama");],
[],
[#NormalTok("response ");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok(" ollama.chat(");],
[#NormalTok("    model");#OperatorTok("=");#StringTok("\"gemma4:e4b\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("    messages");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("[");],
[#NormalTok("        {");#StringTok("\"role\"");#NormalTok(": ");#StringTok("\"system\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"content\"");#NormalTok(": codebook ");#OperatorTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"");#CharTok("\\n");#StringTok("Reply with the theme name only.\"");#NormalTok("},");],
[#NormalTok("        {");#StringTok("\"role\"");#NormalTok(": ");#StringTok("\"user\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"content\"");#NormalTok(": ");#StringTok("\"I cannot afford the tuition fees and rent this term.\"");#NormalTok("}");],
[#NormalTok("    ],");],
[#NormalTok("    options");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("{");#StringTok("\"temperature\"");#NormalTok(": ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok("}");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[#BuiltInTok("print");#NormalTok("(response[");#StringTok("'message'");#NormalTok("][");#StringTok("'content'");#NormalTok("].strip())");],
[#CommentTok("# Output: Finances");],));
For the graduate wellbeing study, all 536 student answers were coded using a local instance of Google Gemma 4 (#NormalTok("gemma4:e4b");) via Ollama. The run script (#NormalTok("data-raw/run_ai_coding.py");) logged the model specifications, execution timestamp, and coded each validation response twice to test stability.

