== Using AI responsibly in academic research
<using-ai-responsibly-in-academic-research>
=== Verify and maintain authorship accountability
<verify-and-maintain-authorship-accountability>
AI tools cannot be listed as authors on academic publications (as confirmed by COPE and major journal publishers) because an AI model cannot take legal or intellectual responsibility for scholarly claims. You remain solely responsible for the accuracy of every finding, citation, and line of code in your thesis.

=== Protect participant data and ethical boundaries
<protect-participant-data-and-ethical-boundaries>
Before transmitting text to cloud-hosted AI APIs, verify that your institutional review board (IRB) ethics approval and participant consent forms explicitly authorize external data sharing. When dealing with sensitive, non-consented, or human-subject data:

- Strip all direct and indirect identifying details (names, institutions, specific dates).
- Utilize #strong[local offline models] (e.g., via Ollama), ensuring zero bytes of text leave your research machine.

=== Maintain computational reproducibility
<maintain-computational-reproducibility>
LLMs are updated continuously by commercial vendors. To ensure reproducible research:

+ Record the exact model identifier (e.g., #NormalTok("gemma4:e4b");), host provider, temperature (#NormalTok("0");), and date.
+ Archive the exact system prompts and codebook files.
+ Save the model's generated outputs as permanent data artifacts (#NormalTok(".csv");), analyzing the frozen outputs rather than querying dynamic endpoints repeatedly.

=== Transparent disclosure
<transparent-disclosure>
Disclose AI assistance transparently in your thesis methodology or acknowledgments:

#quote(block: true)[
#emph["Open-ended challenge responses were qualitatively categorized into seven themes using a local open-weight language model (Gemma 4, e4b variant, via Ollama and Python). Agreement with the primary author's manual coding of a random benchmark sample (N = 200) was validated using Cohen's kappa ($kappa = 0.874$)."]
]

== Common misconceptions
<common-misconceptions-12>
- #strong["High percent agreement proves that qualitative coding is accurate."] High raw agreement often reflects category skew; Cohen's kappa is essential to evaluate agreement above chance expectations.
- #strong["The AI knows the factual answer."] Language models generate plausible linguistic patterns, not verified facts; AI classifications must be validated against human ground truth.
- #strong["Model responses are permanently identical."] Cloud models change across releases and non-zero temperatures introduce stochastic variation; outputs must be archived for reproducibility.
- #strong["Using AI in a thesis is forbidden."] Most academic institutions welcome transparent, disclosed, and validated AI assistance; what is forbidden is unverified, undisclosed, or fabricated content.

== Chapter review
<chapter-review-17>
=== Summary
<summary-17>
- Inter-rater reliability measures agreement between independent coders, corrected for chance using Cohen's kappa ($kappa$).
- Large language models predict sequential tokens based on training patterns; while proficient in Python data workflows, they are prone to confident hallucinations and lack access to external data unless explicitly provided.
- Effective AI assistance requires specific prompts detailing goals, data structures, and target libraries, combined with rigorous manual cross-checking.
- Keyword dictionaries offer a transparent baseline for text classification but fail on complex syntactic context.
- Python interacts with LLMs via official APIs or local Ollama instances; Pydantic ensures reliable structured enum outputs.
- Local LLMs can match human qualitative coding accuracy ($kappa > 0.80$) while preserving participant privacy.
- Responsible AI usage demands human accountability, data protection, deterministic parameters (#NormalTok("temperature=0");), reproducibility archives, and explicit disclosure.

=== Key terms
<key-terms-17>
Qualitative coding, codebook, inter-rater reliability, Cohen's kappa, large language model, token, hallucination, prompt engineering, system prompt, temperature, local model, Ollama, Pydantic, structured output, construct validity, reproducibility, disclosure.

== Exercises
<exercises-17>
The interactive #link("../playground/chapter18.html")[Playground] provides browser-based exercises with solutions:

+ Request an AI assistant to write a pandas aggregation computing average sleep hours by faculty and semester. Test the code on a tiny 3-row DataFrame containing a missing value, and verify that missing data is handled appropriately.
+ Expand the keyword dictionary with three additional regular expressions, and measure whether Cohen's kappa improves on the 200-student test set.
+ Inspect 10 instances where the keyword dictionary disagrees with Elaf's hand coding. Determine whether the discrepancy stems from keyword ambiguity, negation, or multiple mentioned challenges.
+ Classify a sample of 20 open-ended responses using a local language model, and compute the agreement rate with the human benchmark.
+ In the two-coder toy example, modify Coder B so that it assigns 100% of responses to "Workload". Observe the resulting raw agreement and Cohen's kappa.

== Further reading
<further-reading-17>
- Gilardi et al.~(2023), #emph["ChatGPT outperforms crowd-workers for text-annotation tasks"], #emph[PNAS], examining LLM zero-shot classification versus human raters.
- Cohen, J. (1960), #emph["A coefficient of agreement for nominal scales"], #emph[Educational and Psychological Measurement].
- Landis, J. R., & Koch, G. G. (1977), #emph["The measurement of observer agreement for categorical data"], #emph[Biometrics].
- The official Ollama documentation at #link("https://ollama.com")[ollama.com] for running open-weight models locally.

= Putting It All Together
<sec-ch19>
A thesis is an argument, and its results chapter is the evidence. Each claim in it rests on a chain of reasoning that runs from a research question, through a hypothesis, a design, measured variables, and an analysis, to a result, a conclusion, and the limits of that conclusion. A thesis convinces when every link in that chain can be seen and checked, and every number in the text can be traced back to the raw data. The methods of this book are the links; this chapter joins them.

In the story, it is the final semester. Elaf's analyses are spread over two years of scripts, written as she learned each method, and her supervisor's advice for the last stretch is simple: "Before you write the results chapter, rebuild the whole thing, from the raw export to the last table, as one project that runs from start to finish. Then you will know that every number in your thesis is right, and you can answer any examiner's question by pointing to the code." This chapter does exactly that. It sets up a complete research project, runs the analysis from the messy survey export to the tables, figures, and sentences of a results chapter, and traces the chain of reasoning behind three of the research questions. It then steps back: how to report results to the standards journals expect, how to choose a method for a new question, the problems that every researcher meets, and where to go next.

