== Installing Python
<installing-python>
Python is downloaded from the official Python Software Foundation website at #link("https://www.python.org/downloads/")[python.org/downloads]. Always download the latest stable release (version 3.10 or newer; 3.12 is recommended).

=== Windows
<windows>
+ On #link("https://www.python.org/downloads/windows/")[python.org], download the #strong[Windows installer (64-bit)].
+ Run the installer. On the very first screen, #strong[make sure to check the box: "Add python.exe to PATH"]. This is essential: it enables your terminal and code editor to find Python.
+ Click #strong[Install Now]. If prompted with "Disable path length limit" at the end of the installation, click it to avoid Windows file-path truncation issues.

=== macOS
<macos>
+ On #link("https://www.python.org/downloads/macos/")[python.org], download the macOS 64-bit universal installer (#NormalTok(".pkg");).

+ Run the package installer and accept the standard defaults.

+ After installation completes, navigate to your Applications folder, open the #NormalTok("Python 3.x"); folder, and double-click #NormalTok("Install Certificates.command"); to ensure secure network connections (SSL) function properly when downloading datasets and packages.

+ Alternatively, if you use Homebrew, you can install Python directly from Terminal:

  #Skylighting(([#ExtensionTok("brew");#NormalTok(" install python");],));

=== Linux
<linux>
Python comes pre-installed on most modern Linux distributions. On Debian/Ubuntu distributions, ensure the development packages and package manager are installed:

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("sudo");#NormalTok(" apt update");],
[#FunctionTok("sudo");#NormalTok(" apt install python3 python3-pip python3-venv");],));
=== Checking the installation
<checking-the-installation>
Open your system terminal (Command Prompt or PowerShell on Windows; Terminal on macOS and Linux) and type:

#Skylighting(([#ExtensionTok("python");#NormalTok(" ");#AttributeTok("--version");],));
If it prints #NormalTok("Python 3.12.x"); (or any version 3.10+), Python is installed and accessible.

== Installing VS Code
<installing-vs-code>
+ Download #strong[Visual Studio Code] from #link("https://code.visualstudio.com/")[code.visualstudio.com] for your operating system.
