== Managing projects with virtual environments
<managing-projects-with-virtual-environments>
In empirical research, package versions evolve. To prevent package updates from inadvertently altering your thesis calculations months later, create a #strong[virtual environment] for your research project. A virtual environment is an isolated directory containing its own Python interpreter and set of installed libraries.

In your research project folder:

#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# 1. Create a virtual environment named .venv");],
[#ExtensionTok("python");#NormalTok(" ");#AttributeTok("-m");#NormalTok(" venv .venv");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# 2. Activate the virtual environment");],
[#CommentTok("# Windows (PowerShell):");],
[#ExtensionTok(".venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1");],
[#CommentTok("# Windows (Command Prompt):");],
[#ExtensionTok(".venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat");],
[#CommentTok("# macOS / Linux:");],
[#BuiltInTok("source");#NormalTok(" .venv/bin/activate");],));
When activated, #NormalTok("(.venv)"); appears at the start of your terminal command line. In VS Code, press #NormalTok("Ctrl+Shift+P"); (or #NormalTok("Cmd+Shift+P");), type #NormalTok("Python: Select Interpreter");, and choose the interpreter inside your #NormalTok(".venv"); directory.

