== Common problems and solutions
<common-problems-and-solutions>
/ #strong[#NormalTok("'python' is not recognized as an internal or external command");]: #block[
Python was installed without checking "Add python.exe to PATH". Re-run the Python Windows installer, select #strong[Modify], and check the PATH option, or add your Python installation folder (e.g., #NormalTok("C:\\Users\\Username\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\Python\\Python312\\");) to your system Environment Variables.
]

/ #strong[#NormalTok("ModuleNotFoundError: No module named '...'");]: #block[
The library is not installed in the currently active Python environment, or VS Code is pointing to a different Python interpreter. Open the VS Code Command Palette (#NormalTok("Ctrl+Shift+P");), select #NormalTok("Python: Select Interpreter");, and choose your project's #NormalTok(".venv");. Then run #NormalTok("pip install <package-name>");.
]

/ #strong[#NormalTok("Execution of scripts is disabled on this system (Windows PowerShell)");]: #block[
PowerShell on Windows restricts script execution by default. Open PowerShell as Administrator and run:
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("Set-ExecutionPolicy");#NormalTok(" ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok("ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned ");#OperatorTok("-");#NormalTok("Scope CurrentUser");],));
This allows activating local virtual environment scripts.
]

/ #strong[#NormalTok("File not found"); when loading datasets]: #block[
In research scripts, always use relative paths starting from the project root (e.g., #NormalTok("data/students.csv");), or use the helper function #NormalTok("dt.load_students()");. Avoid absolute paths like #NormalTok("C:\\Users\\Elaf\\Desktop\\data.csv");, which fail when shared with supervisors or collaborators.
]

/ #strong[Restarting a clean session]: #block[
If variables from earlier interactive runs are causing unexpected errors, restart the Python interactive kernel. In VS Code, click the #strong[Restart] button at the top of the Interactive Window or notebook.
]

= Appendix B: Glossary
<sec-app-glossary>
This appendix collects the key terms from every chapter's review, with a plain-language definition of each and the chapters where it is introduced or applied, followed by a reference table of the Python functions and methods used most frequently in the book.

