=== The core scientific stack
<the-core-scientific-stack>
Rather than requiring dozens of disparate packages, scientific Python is built on a coherent, tightly integrated stack:

+ #strong[#NormalTok("pandas"); and #NormalTok("numpy");]: The foundation for tabular data structures and numerical operations. A DataFrame is the Python counterpart of a spreadsheet or database table, with support for filtering, grouping, joining, and missing-value imputation.
+ #strong[#NormalTok("matplotlib"); and #NormalTok("seaborn");]: The standard libraries for visualization. Matplotlib provides low-level graphical rendering and layout control, while Seaborn offers high-level statistical plotting routines (histograms, regression plots, and boxplots).
+ #strong[#NormalTok("scipy"); and #NormalTok("statsmodels");]: The statistical modeling core. Scipy provides probability distributions and classical hypothesis testing ($t$-tests, chi-square, Mann-Whitney), while Statsmodels handles econometric and regression models with comprehensive diagnostic tables (OLS, logistic regression, ANOVA, and linear mixed-effects).
+ #strong[#NormalTok("scikit-learn");]: The premier machine learning toolkit for empirical research, providing consistent APIs (#NormalTok("fit");, #NormalTok("transform");, #NormalTok("predict");) for supervised classification, regression, clustering, and cross-validation pipelines.

= Appendix D: Common Errors and How to Fix Them
<sec-app-errors>
Everyone who writes Python code encounters error messages on a regular basis---experts included. An error is not a sign of failure; it is Python explaining, as precisely as possible, what went wrong and where execution stopped.

This appendix collects the exceptions and pitfalls that researchers are most likely to encounter in empirical analysis, what each message means, and how to fix it. The examples below are live: each was executed when compiling the book, so the tracebacks match exactly what your Python environment will display.

== Anatomy of a Python traceback
<anatomy-of-a-python-traceback>
When Python cannot execute an instruction, it raises an #strong[exception] and prints a #strong[traceback].

+ #strong[Read from bottom to top:] The very last line of the traceback displays the exception type and the specific message explaining what failed (for example, #NormalTok("KeyError: 'age_years'");).
+ #strong[Find your code:] Look upward through the traceback to find the last line that points to your script file (e.g., #NormalTok("analysis.py, line 24");). That is the exact line where execution halted.
+ #strong[Warnings vs.~Exceptions:] An exception terminates execution immediately. A #strong[warning] (such as pandas' #NormalTok("SettingWithCopyWarning"); or #NormalTok("FutureWarning");) allows the script to continue running, but alerts you to a potential issue or deprecated syntax.

